{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07167","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07167","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07167","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lekang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Lekang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjun","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Wenjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephan","familyName":"Goetz","name":"Goetz, Stephan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning for Hierarchical Text Classification: The Case of Patents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:06:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:57:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-17T07:51:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-20T00:38:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hierarchical text classification (HTC) assigns documents to multiple levels of a pre-defined taxonomy. Automated patent subject classification represents one of the hardest HTC scenarios because of professional difficulties and extensive labels. Prior approaches only output a flat label set, which offers little insight into the reason behind predictions. Therefore, we propose Reasoning for Hierarchical Classification (RHC), a novel framework that reformulates HTC as a step-by-step reasoning task to sequentially deduce hierarchical labels. RHC trains large language models (LLMs) in two stages: a cold-start stage that aligns outputs with chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning format and a reinforcement learning (RL) stage to enhance multi-step reasoning ability. RHC demonstrates four advantages in our experiments. (1) Effectiveness: RHC surpasses previous baselines and outperforms the supervised fine-tuning counterparts by approximately 3% in accuracy and macro F1. (2) Explainability: RHC produces natural-language justifications before prediction to facilitate human inspection. (3) Scalability: RHC scales favorably with model size with larger gains compared to standard fine-tuning. (4) Applicability: Beyond patents, we further demonstrate that RHC achieves state-of-the-art performance on other widely used HTC benchmarks, which highlights its broad applicability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026 Findings. 16 pages, 10 tables, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07167","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:28Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:15:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07231","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07231","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07231","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donggyu","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Donggyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeok","familyName":"Yun","name":"Yun, Hyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meeyoung","familyName":"Cha","name":"Cha, Meeyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungwon","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Sungwon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangyoon","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Sangyoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihee","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jihee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EconCausal: A Context-Aware Economic Reasoning Benchmark for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:00:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:00:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T16:46:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:08:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-23T05:21:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-24T02:10:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-26T12:27:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:56:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-14T04:49:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-10-15T00:40:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Socio-economic causal effects depend heavily on their institutional and environmental contexts. The same intervention can produce different, even opposite, effects across regulatory regimes, market conditions, time periods, or populations. This poses a challenge for large language models (LLMs) in decision-support roles: can they infer the direction of a causal effect under a specified context, and revise that judgment when the context changes?\n To address this, we introduce EconCausal, a large-scale benchmark of 10,490 context-annotated causal triplets extracted from 2,595 high-quality empirical studies in top-tier economics and finance journals, constructed through a rigorous four-stage pipeline with multi-run consensus, context refinement, and multi-critic filtering.\n Across 19 models, we observe source-sign over-transfer in cross-context prediction. While top models reach ~84% accuracy in single-context prediction, mean accuracy is 74.75% overall but drops to 44.27% when source and target signs disagree. On these cases, accuracy is 11.54 percentage points (pp) lower with the signed source example than with the target input alone. Mean accuracy is below 60% under corrupted source signs. Models also struggle to predict study-reported nonsignificant effects (19.16% accuracy).\n The dataset and benchmark are publicly available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/econcausal-benchmark-6F12."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages. Revised version with updated evaluation results, robustness analyses, expert validation, and references"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07231","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:04:58Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:04:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-15T03:09:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06225","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06225","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06225","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yining","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziteng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yirui","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Junyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Shuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yike","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalized Multi-agent Social Simulation Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T09:36:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-14T04:43:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-15T00:39:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent social interaction has clearly benefited from Large Language Models. However, current simulation systems still face challenges such as difficulties in scaling to diverse scenarios and poor reusability due to a lack of modular design. To address these issues, we designed and developed a modular, object-oriented framework that organically integrates various base classes through a hierarchical structure, harvesting scalability and reusability. We inherited the framework to realize common derived classes. Additionally, a memory summarization mechanism is proposed to filter and distill relevant information from raw memory data, prioritizing contextually salient events and interactions. By selecting and combining some necessary derived classes, we customized a specific simulated environment. Utilizing this simulated environment, we successfully simulated human interactions on social media, replicating real-world online social behaviors. The source code for the project will be released and evolve."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06225","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:20Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-15T03:09:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06270","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06270","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06270","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nian","familyName":"Ran","name":"Ran, Nian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongzheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhongzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingsong","familyName":"Ran","name":"Ran, Qingsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shikun","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Shikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Allmendinger","name":"Allmendinger, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoguang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xiaoguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MCCE: A Framework for Multi-LLM Collaborative Search in Discrete Spaces with Similarity-Filtered Preference Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T10:03:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-27T07:42:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:34:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-objective discrete optimization problems, such as molecular design, pose significant challenges due to their vast and unstructured combinatorial spaces. Traditional evolutionary algorithms often get trapped in local optima, while expert knowledge can provide crucial guidance for accelerating convergence. Large language models (LLMs) offer powerful priors and reasoning ability, making them natural optimizers when expert knowledge matters. However, closed-source LLMs, though strong in exploration, cannot update their parameters and thus cannot internalize experience. Conversely, smaller open models can be continually fine-tuned but lack broad knowledge and reasoning strength. We introduce Multi-LLM Collaborative Co-evolution (MCCE), a hybrid framework that unites a frozen closed-source LLM with a lightweight trainable model. The system maintains a trajectory memory of past search processes; the small model is progressively refined via reinforcement learning, with the two models jointly supporting and complementing each other in global exploration. Unlike model distillation, this process enhances the capabilities of both models through mutual inspiration. Experiments on multi-objective drug design benchmarks show that MCCE achieves state-of-the-art Pareto front quality and consistently outperforms baselines. These results highlight a new paradigm for enabling continual evolution in hybrid LLM systems, combining knowledge-driven exploration with experience-driven learning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06270","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:22Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:40:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06607","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06607","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06607","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weidi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Weidi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaogeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaogeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hung-Chun","familyName":"Chiu","name":"Chiu, Hung-Chun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xusheng","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Xusheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinzhi","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yinzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaowei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Chaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Your Harness is Not Secure: Benchmarking Real-world Threat of Command Line Interface Agent"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T03:35:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:21:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T18:18:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:04:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-21T05:20:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-24T00:21:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Command-line interface (CLI) agents powered by large language models (LLMs) can interpret natural-language requests, plan multi-step tasks, execute shell commands, and modify files and system state. As these agents are increasingly used for operating-system (OS) workflows, it is important to evaluate whether they can be misused to carry out security-relevant operations. Existing benchmarks often lack an attacker-knowledge model grounded in tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs), provide limited coverage of end-to-end kill chains, rely on simplified single-host environments, or use LLM-as-a-judge for success evaluation. We introduce AdvCLI, an MITRE ATT\u0026amp;CK-aligned benchmark for evaluating OS-level misuse risks of CLI agents in a controlled multi-host sandbox. AdvCLI contains 140 tasks: 40 direct malicious requests, 74 TTP-based tasks, and 26 end-to-end kill chains. Each task is paired with deterministic hard-coded verification protocols that check whether the requested OS-level effect is realized. We evaluate seven CLI agents and products built on nine foundation models, including ReAct, OpenClaw, OpenAI Agent SDK, Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Cursor CLI, and Cursor IDE. Results show that current CLI agents frequently proceed beyond refusal and can complete a non-negligible fraction of malicious OS-level tasks, especially when requests include TTP-style attacker knowledge. AdvCLI provides a reproducible testbed for evaluating these risks and for developing stronger safety mechanisms for tool-using CLI agents in the future."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EMNLP 2026 (Findings)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06607","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:50:11Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:50:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-24T02:23:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06717","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06717","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06717","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanfei","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yuanfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Illing","name":"Illing, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Althoff","name":"Althoff, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SanDRA: Safe Large-Language-Model-Based Decision Making for Automated Vehicles Using Reachability Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:13:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:29:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-11T02:10:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-14T00:16:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have been widely applied to knowledge-driven decision-making for automated vehicles due to their strong generalization and reasoning capabilities. However, the safety of the resulting decisions cannot be ensured due to possible hallucinations and the lack of integrated vehicle dynamics. To address this issue, we propose SanDRA, the first safe large-language-model-based decision making framework for automated vehicles using reachability analysis. Our approach starts with a comprehensive description of the driving scenario to prompt LLMs to generate and rank feasible driving actions. These actions are translated into temporal logic formulas that incorporate formalized traffic rules, and are subsequently integrated into reachability analysis to eliminate unsafe actions. We validate our approach in both open-loop and closed-loop driving environments using off-the-shelf and finetuned LLMs, showing that it can provide provably safe and, where possible, legally compliant driving actions, even under high-density traffic conditions. To ensure transparency and facilitate future research, all code and experimental setups are publicly available at github.com/CommonRoad/SanDRA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"@2026 IEEE. Personal use of this material is permitted. Permission from IEEE must be obtained for all other uses, in any current or future media, including reprinting/republishing this material for advertising or promotional purposes, creating new collective works, for resale or redistribution to servers or lists, or reuse of any copyrighted component of this work in other works"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06717","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:52:48Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:52:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-14T03:58:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06288","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06288","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06288","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raj","familyName":"Ghugare","name":"Ghugare, Raj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roger Creus","familyName":"Castanyer","name":"Castanyer, Roger Creus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Catherine","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Catherine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kathryn","familyName":"Wantlin","name":"Wantlin, Kathryn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Schofield","name":"Schofield, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthik","familyName":"Narasimhan","name":"Narasimhan, Karthik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Eysenbach","name":"Eysenbach, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BuilderBench: The Building Blocks of Intelligent Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T04:23:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-23T02:52:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:25:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:36:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-25T00:23:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-02T13:49:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-03T00:52:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Today's AI models learn primarily through mimicry and refining, so it is not surprising that they struggle to solve problems beyond the limits set by existing data. To solve novel problems, agents should acquire skills by exploring and learning through experience. Finding a scalable learning mechanism for developing agents that learn through interaction remains a major open problem. In this work, we introduce BuilderBench, a benchmark to accelerate research into agent training that centers open-ended exploration. BuilderBench requires agents to learn how to build any structure using blocks. BuilderBench is equipped with (1) a simulator of a robot interacting with various physical blocks, and (2) a task-suite with over 50 diverse target structures that are carefully curated to test an understanding of physics, mathematics, and long-horizon planning. Agents are provided with a target structure at the start, and can interact with the environment for multiple episodes to experiment and learn various skills for building the structure. Solving these tasks requires \\emph{embodied reasoning} in a way that is not reflected in words but rather in actions, experimenting with different strategies and piecing them together. Our experiments with multiple state-of-the-art frontier language model based agents and tabula rasa reinforcement learning algorithms show that these agents cannot solve any of the non-trivial tasks in the BuilderBench. Our analysis throws light on the lack of exploration abilities in these models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Blogpost: https://rajghugare19.github.io/builderbench and Code: https://github.com/rajghugare19/builderbench"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06288","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:47Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-03T02:13:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06452","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06452","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06452","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinsheng","familyName":"Ba","name":"Ba, Jinsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sverrir","familyName":"Thorgeirsson","name":"Thorgeirsson, Sverrir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhendong","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zhendong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Code Semantic Zooming"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T20:43:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:08:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-05T09:42:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-06T00:35:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-30T19:34:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-02T00:02:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) have introduced a new paradigm for software development, where source code is generated from natural language prompts. While this paradigm significantly boosts development productivity, building complex, real-world software systems remains challenging because natural language offers limited control over the code generation process. Inspired by the historical evolution of programming languages toward higher levels of abstraction, we advocate for a high-level abstraction language that gives developers greater control over LLM-assisted code writing. To this end, we propose Code Semantic Zooming (CodeZoom), a novel approach based on pseudocode that allows developers to iteratively explore, understand, and refine code across multiple layers of semantic abstraction. In a within-subjects user study (n=26), our method matches a state-of-the-art coding agent, Claude Code, on usability while producing a large effect on code comprehension: over 90% of participants reported feeling more in control of design decisions when using CodeZoom compared to using Claude Code."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06452","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:46:37Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:46:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-02T02:11:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06732","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06732","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06732","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiancheng","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Tiancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jerry","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jerry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are LLMs Reliable Rankers? Rank Manipulation via Two-Stage Token Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:40:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:31:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-28T18:43:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-30T01:13:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used as rerankers in information retrieval, yet their ranking behavior can be steered by small, natural-sounding prompts. To expose this vulnerability, we present Rank Anything First (RAF), a two-stage token optimization method that crafts concise textual perturbations to consistently promote a target item in LLM-generated rankings while remaining hard to detect. Stage 1 uses Greedy Coordinate Gradient to shortlist candidate tokens at the current position by combining the gradient of the rank-target with a readability score; Stage 2 evaluates those candidates under exact ranking and readability losses using an entropy-based dynamic weighting scheme, and selects a token via temperature-controlled sampling. RAF generates ranking-promoting prompts token-by-token, guided by dual objectives: maximizing ranking effectiveness and preserving linguistic naturalness. Experiments across multiple LLMs show that RAF significantly boosts the rank of target items using naturalistic language, with greater robustness than existing methods in both promoting target items and maintaining naturalness. These findings underscore a critical security implication: LLM-based reranking is inherently susceptible to adversarial manipulation, raising new challenges for the trustworthiness and robustness of modern retrieval systems. Our code is available at: https://github.com/glad-lab/RAF."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06732","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:09Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-30T04:07:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06445","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06445","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06445","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asif","familyName":"Shahriar","name":"Shahriar, Asif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Nafiu","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Md Nafiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sadif","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Sadif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farig","familyName":"Sadeque","name":"Sadeque, Farig","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Rizwan","familyName":"Parvez","name":"Parvez, Md Rizwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Survey on Agentic Security: Applications, Threats and Defenses"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T20:32:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:08:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-20T13:42:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-23T01:27:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-12T21:57:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-16T00:12:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM-based agents are now used throughout cybersecurity. While these agents facilitate powerful and autonomous security applications, their autonomy opens up new attack surfaces, and the security community is actively building defenses to secure them. Yet the literature on this subject has grown quickly and unevenly. Existing surveys treat applications, threats, and defenses in isolation, leaving no unified account of how an agent's capabilities, vulnerabilities, and countermeasures interconnect. In this work we present the first holistic survey of the agentic security landscape, structuring the field around the fundamental pillars of Applications, Threats and Defenses. We provide a comprehensive taxonomy of over 260 papers, explaining how agents are used in downstream cybersecurity applications, inherent threats to agentic systems, and countermeasures designed to protect them. In addition, we provide detailed pillar-specific and cross-cutting analyses that show the security-lifecycle coverage of agentic applications, comparison between red-teaming and blue-teaming agents, and the adversarial use of red-teaming applications. On the threat side, we analyze the entry points and agent-loop stages that attacks target, their specificity to the agentic setting, and the threat models they assume. On the defense side, we analyze the prevailing defense strategies, their cost and security trade-offs, and where in the agent lifecycle they are deployed. We further map which defenses cover which attack classes and chart trends in agent architecture, backbone model usage, data modality coverage, and the growth of attack and defense research over time. Taken together, these findings indicate that agentic systems are structurally fragile by default and that securing them will require defenses that span the full agent lifecycle rather than single-layer fixes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06445","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:46:27Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:46:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-16T04:06:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06843","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06843","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06843","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuhang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zhifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deyi","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Deyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lanyun","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Lanyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-signals Driven Multi-LLM Debate for Efficient and Accurate Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:10:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:38:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-26T13:10:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:58:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have exhibited impressive capabilities across diverse application domains. Recent work has explored Multi-LLM Agent Debate (MAD) as a way to enhance performance by enabling multiple LLMs to discuss and refine responses iteratively. Nevertheless, existing MAD methods predominantly focus on utilizing external structures, such as debate graphs, using LLM-as-a-Judge, while neglecting the application of self signals, such as token logits and attention, that arise during generation. This omission leads to redundant computation and potential performance degradation. In this paper, we shift the focus to the self signals of multi-LLM debate and introduce a Self-Signals Driven Multi-LLM Debate (SID), which leverages two types of self-signals: model-level confidence and token-level semantic focus, to adaptively guide the debate process. Our approach enables high-confidence agents to exit early at the model level and compress the redundant debate contents based on the attention mechanism. We evaluate our method on various LLMs and Multimodal LLMs across multiple challenging benchmarks. Experimental results demonstrate that our method not only outperforms existing MAD techniques in accuracy but also reduces token consumption, highlighting the effectiveness of utilizing self signals in enhancing both the performance and efficiency of multi-agent debate systems. Our code will be available at~\\href{https://github.com/xuhang2019/SID}{\\texttt{https://github.com/xuhang2019/SID}}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06843","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:45Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:36:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06672","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06672","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06672","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Udbhav","familyName":"Bamba","name":"Bamba, Udbhav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghao","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Minghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haizhong","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Haizhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"XRPO: Pushing the limits of GRPO with Targeted Exploration and Exploitation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:53:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:26:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:39:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:19:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-25T01:59:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-26T01:20:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning algorithms such as GRPO have driven recent advances in large language model (LLM) reasoning. While scaling the number of rollouts stabilizes training, existing approaches suffer from limited exploration on challenging prompts and leave informative feedback signals underexploited, due to context-independent rollout allocation across prompts (e.g., generating 16 rollouts per prompt) and relying heavily on sparse rewards. This paper presents XRPO(eXplore - eXploit GRPO), a unified framework that recasts policy optimization through the principled lens of rollout exploration-exploitation. To enhance exploration, XRPO introduces a mathematically grounded rollout allocator that adaptively prioritizes prompts with higher potential for uncertainty reduction. It further addresses stagnation on zero-reward prompts through an in-context seeding strategy that injects curated exemplars, steering the model into more difficult reasoning trajectories. To strengthen exploitation, XRPO develops a group-relative, novelty-aware advantage sharpening mechanism that leverages sequence likelihoods to amplify low-probability yet correct responses, thereby extending the policy's reach beyond sparse rewards. Experiments across diverse math and coding benchmarks on both reasoning and non-reasoning models demonstrate that XRPO outperforms existing advances (e.g., GRPO and GSPO) up to 4% pass@1 and 6% cons@32, while accelerating training convergence by up to 2.7X."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06672","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:45Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-26T04:04:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06824","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06824","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06824","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linus","familyName":"Kreitner","name":"Kreitner, Linus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Hager","name":"Hager, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Mengedoht","name":"Mengedoht, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgios","familyName":"Kaissis","name":"Kaissis, Georgios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Rueckert","name":"Rueckert, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin J.","familyName":"Menten","name":"Menten, Martin J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient numeracy in language models through single-token number embeddings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:48:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:37:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-20T14:19:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-21T01:03:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To drive progress in science and engineering, large language models (LLMs) must be able to process large amounts of numerical data and solve long calculations efficiently. This is currently only possible through the use of external tools or extensive reasoning chains, either weakening the numerical representations of LLMs or limiting the length of problems they can solve. We show that frontier LLMs require excessive amounts of reasoning tokens to solve even basic calculations, which is exacerbated by their tokenization strategies that split single numbers into multiple tokens. This motivates the need for efficient and effective single-token number encodings. We introduce a set of desiderata for such encodings and show that existing approaches fail to fulfill them. To address these shortcomings, we propose BitTokens, a novel encoding strategy that represents any number as a single token using its IEEE 754 binary floating-point representation. Through extensive experiments we show that our BitTokens allow even small language models to learn algorithms that solve basic arithmetic operations nearly perfectly. This newly gained efficiency could expand the length and complexity of problems language models can solve."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06824","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:18Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-21T02:35:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06719","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06719","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06719","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junki","familyName":"Mori","name":"Mori, Junki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuya","familyName":"Kakizaki","name":"Kakizaki, Kazuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taiki","familyName":"Miyagawa","name":"Miyagawa, Taiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Sakuma","name":"Sakuma, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Differentially Private Synthetic Text Generation for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:15:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:29:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-12T15:11:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-13T01:14:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) enhances large language models (LLMs) by grounding them in external knowledge. However, its application in sensitive domains is limited by privacy risks. Existing private RAG methods typically rely on query-time differential privacy (DP), which requires repeated noise injection and leads to accumulated privacy loss. To address this issue, we propose DP-SynRAG, a framework that uses LLMs to generate differentially private synthetic RAG databases. Unlike prior methods, the synthetic text can be reused once created, thereby avoiding repeated noise injection and additional privacy costs. To preserve essential information for downstream RAG tasks, DP-SynRAG extends private prediction, which instructs LLMs to generate text that mimics subsampled database records in a DP manner. Experiments show that DP-SynRAG achieves superior performance to the state-of-the-art private RAG systems while maintaining a fixed privacy budget, offering a scalable solution for privacy-preserving RAG."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACL 2026 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06719","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:52:51Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:52:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:57:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06371","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06371","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06371","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Firoj","familyName":"Alam","name":"Alam, Firoj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali Ezzat","familyName":"Shahroor","name":"Shahroor, Ali Ezzat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md. Arid","familyName":"Hasan","name":"Hasan, Md. Arid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zien Sheikh","familyName":"Ali","name":"Ali, Zien Sheikh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hunzalah Hassan","familyName":"Bhatti","name":"Bhatti, Hunzalah Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed Bayan","familyName":"Kmainasi","name":"Kmainasi, Mohamed Bayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shammur Absar","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Shammur Absar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Basel","familyName":"Mousi","name":"Mousi, Basel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fahim","familyName":"Dalvi","name":"Dalvi, Fahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nadir","familyName":"Durrani","name":"Durrani, Nadir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natasa","familyName":"Milic-Frayling","name":"Milic-Frayling, Natasa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OASIS: A Multilingual and Multimodal Dataset for Culturally Grounded Spoken Visual QA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"F.2.2; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:37:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:04:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-09T06:32:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T00:32:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T05:22:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-13T00:35:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale multimodal models achieve strong results on tasks like Visual Question Answering (VQA), but they are often limited when queries require cultural and visual information, everyday knowledge, particularly in low-resource and underrepresented languages. We introduce OASIS, a large-scale culturally grounded multimodal QA dataset covering images, text, and speech. OASIS is built with EverydayMMQA, a scalable semi-automatic framework for creating localized spoken and visual QA resources, supported by multi-stage human-in-the-loop validation. OASIS contains approximately 0.92M real images and 14.8M QA pairs, including 3.7M spoken questions, with 383 hours of human-recorded speech, and 20K hours of voice-cloned speech, from 42 speakers. It supports four input settings: text-only, speech-only, text+image, and speech+image. The dataset focuses on English and Arabic varieties across 18 countries, covering Modern Standard Arabic (MSA) as well as dialectal Arabic. It is designed to evaluate models beyond object recognition, targeting pragmatic, commonsense, and culturally grounded reasoning in real-world scenarios. We benchmark four closed-source models, three open-source models, and one fine-tuned model on OASIS. The framework and dataset will be made publicly available to the community. https://huggingface.co/datasets/QCRI/OASIS"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Multimodal Foundation Models, Large Language Models, Native, Multilingual, Language Diversity, Contextual Understanding, Culturally Informed"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06371","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:44Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:57:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07248","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07248","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07248","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonggeun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jonggeun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Woojung","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Woojung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongwook","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jongwook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haesung","familyName":"Pyun","name":"Pyun, Haesung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yohan","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Yohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Don't Adapt Small Language Models for Tools; Adapt Tool Schemas to the Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:16:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:01:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-07T09:46:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-08T01:32:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-19T15:11:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:15:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Small language models (SLMs) enable scalable tool-augmented multi-agent systems where multiple SLMs handle subtasks orchestrated by a powerful coordinator. However, they struggle with tool-use tasks, particularly in selecting appropriate tools and identifying correct parameters. A common failure mode is \\textit{schema misalignment}: models hallucinate plausible tool names that are absent from the provided tool schema, due to different naming conventions internalized during pretraining. Rather than training models to adapt to unfamiliar schemas, we propose adapting schemas to align with models' pretrained knowledge. We introduce \\textbf{PA-Tool} (Pretraining-Aligned Tool Schema Generation), a training-free method that leverages peakedness, a signal used in contamination detection that indicates pretraining familiarity, to rename tool components. By generating multiple candidates and selecting the candidate with the highest peakedness, PA-Tool identifies pretraining-aligned naming patterns. Experiments on MetaTool and RoTBench show improvements of up to 17\\%, with schema misalignment errors reduced by 80\\%. PA-Tool enables small models to substantially improve tool-use accuracy without retraining, showing that schema-level interventions can unlock the tool-use potential of resource-efficient models. Our code is available at https://github.com/holi-lab/PA-Tool."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL 2026 (Main)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07248","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:23Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07143","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07143","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07143","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenfei","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Chenfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wensong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wensong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichen","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haocong","familyName":"He","name":"He, Haocong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanhuiyi","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Yuanhuiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lutao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Lutao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqian","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yuqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuming","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are We Using the Right Benchmark: An Evaluation Framework for Visual Token Compression Methods"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:44:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:56:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-23T06:35:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-26T01:20:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T07:39:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:47:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent efforts to accelerate inference in Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have largely focused on visual token compression. The effectiveness of these methods is commonly evaluated by measuring the accuracy drop on existing MLLM benchmarks before and after compression. However, these benchmarks are originally designed to assess general perception and reasoning abilities, rather than the specific challenges posed by visual token compression, leading to a fundamental task mismatch. In this work, we uncover a counterintuitive yet consistent phenomenon: simple image downsampling outperforms many advanced visual token compression methods across multiple widely used benchmarks. Through a comprehensive empirical study spanning eight popular benchmarks and multiple state-of-the-art compression techniques, we show that (i) current benchmarks contain substantial noise (task-irrelevant samples) for evaluating visual token compression, and (ii) downsampling can act as an effective data filter that distinguishes between simple and difficult samples with respect to compression sensitivity. Motivated by these findings, we propose VTC-Bench, an evaluation framework that explicitly leverages downsampling as a discriminator to denoise existing benchmarks, enabling a fairer and more meaningful additional assessment of visual token compression methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACL2026 Main. Code: https://github.com/Chenfei-Liao/VTC-Bench; Project Page: https://chenfei-liao.github.io/VTC-Bench-Page/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07143","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:02:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:02:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06296","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06296","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06296","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingfei","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Lingfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengdi","familyName":"Che","name":"Che, Fengdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuhan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binhang","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Binhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VeriEquivBench: An Equivalence Score for Ground-Truth-Free Evaluation of Formally Verifiable Code"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:19:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-19T09:53:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-20T00:47:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-18T14:30:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T00:45:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Formal verification is the next frontier for ensuring the correctness of code generated by Large Language Models (LLMs). While methods that co-generate code and formal specifications in formal languages, like Dafny, can, in principle, prove alignment with user intent, progress is bottlenecked by specification quality evaluation. Current benchmarks rely on matching against ground-truth specifications, a manual and expertise-intensive process that has limited existing datasets to a few hundred simple problems and also suffers from a reliability issue. To address this, we introduce VeriEquivBench, a new benchmark with $2,389$ complex algorithmic problems that probe the limitations of current models in both code generation and formal reasoning. Our evaluation framework replaces ground-truth matching with a formally grounded metric, the equivalence score, and rigorously verifies the quality of generated specifications and code. Our results show that generating formally verifiable code remains a profound challenge for state-of-the-art LLMs. This underscores both the difficulty of the task and the need for benchmarks like VeriEquivBench to drive progress toward scalable and reliable coding agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06296","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:59Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:43:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06700","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06700","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06700","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonardo","familyName":"Bertolazzi","name":"Bertolazzi, Leonardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandro","familyName":"Pezzelle","name":"Pezzelle, Sandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raffaella","familyName":"Bernardi","name":"Bernardi, Raffaella","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Language Models Conflate Logical Validity with Plausibility: A Representational Analysis of Content Effects"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T06:48:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:28:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T09:31:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:18:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T07:21:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:46:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Both humans and large language models (LLMs) exhibit content effects: biases in which the plausibility of the semantic content of a reasoning problem influences judgments regarding its logical validity. While this phenomenon in humans is best explained by the dual-process theory of reasoning, the mechanisms behind content effects in LLMs remain unclear. In this work, we address this issue by investigating how LLMs encode the concepts of validity and plausibility within their internal representations. We show that both concepts are linearly represented and strongly aligned in representational geometry, leading models to conflate plausibility with validity. Using steering vectors, we demonstrate that plausibility vectors can causally bias validity judgements, and vice versa, and that the degree of alignment between these two concepts predicts the magnitude of behavioral content effects across models. Finally, we construct debiasing vectors that disentangle these concepts, reducing content effects and improving reasoning accuracy. Our findings advance understanding of how abstract logical concepts are represented in LLMs and highlight representational interventions as a path toward more logical systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06700","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:52:24Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:52:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07043","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07043","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07043","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting-Yao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Ting-Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raviteja","familyName":"Vemulapalli","name":"Vemulapalli, Raviteja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hema Swetha","familyName":"Koppula","name":"Koppula, Hema Swetha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mert","familyName":"Cemri","name":"Cemri, Mert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiarui","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jiarui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"COMPASS: Benchmarking Constrained Optimization in LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:09:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:50:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-16T19:10:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T00:05:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human decision-making often involves constrained optimization. As LLM agents are deployed to assist with real-world tasks like travel planning, shopping, and scheduling, they must mirror this capability. We introduce COMPASS, a benchmark that evaluates whether LLM agents can perform constrained optimization in realistic travel planning settings. To success in these tasks, agents must engage in multi-turn conversations with user to gather task information as well as use tools to gather information from the database. Then agents must propose a solution that not only satisfies hard constraints but also optimizes user's utility objective. Evaluating state-of-the-art models, we reveal a significant feasible-optimal gap: while models achieve 70-90% feasibility (constraint satisfaction), they reach only 20-60% optimality (utility optimization). Our analysis shows that tool use is not the bottleneck. Instead, the core limitation is insufficient exploration of the search space, with success strongly correlating with information gathered. Coding agents show a promising approach to mitigate this gap. Together, COMPASS provides a testbed for developing LLM agents that can truly mirror human decision-making by both satisfying constraints and optimizing objectives."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07043","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:30Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-20T02:09:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06953","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06953","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06953","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minju","familyName":"Gwak","name":"Gwak, Minju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guijin","familyName":"Son","name":"Son, Guijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaehyung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jaehyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Revisiting the Uniform Information Density Hypothesis in LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T12:37:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:45:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-14T03:29:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:14:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-17T04:43:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-20T00:25:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Uniform Information Density (UID) hypothesis proposes that effective communication is achieved by maintaining a stable flow of information. In this work, we revisit this principle in the context of Large Language Model (LLM) reasoning, asking whether step-level uniformity reflects reasoning quality. To this end, we introduce a novel framework to quantify uniformity of information flow at both local and global levels, using an entropy-based stepwise density metric. Across experiments on seven reasoning benchmarks, we see a counter-intuitive pattern: while high-quality reasoning exhibit smooth step-by-step transitions local uniformity and structured, non-uniform information flow at the trajectory level global non-uniformity. The results demonstrate that these uniformities outperform alternative internal signals as predictors of reasoning quality, and such divergence with human communication is not a model deficiency, but a byproduct of distinct objectives between human communication and LLM reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06953","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:58:19Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:58:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-20T02:09:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07019","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07019","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07019","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jusen","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Jusen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxi","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jiaxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weigao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Weigao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Native Hybrid Attention for Efficient Sequence Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:44:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:48:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T09:31:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:31:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-15T13:24:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-16T00:50:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers excel at sequence modeling but face quadratic complexity, while linear attention offers improved efficiency but often compromises recall accuracy over long contexts. In this work, we introduce Native Hybrid Attention (NHA), a novel hybrid architecture of linear and full attention that integrates both intra \u0026amp; inter-layer hybridization into a unified layer design. NHA maintains long-term context in key-value slots updated by a linear RNN, and augments them with short-term tokens from a sliding window. A single softmax attention operation is then applied over all keys and values, enabling per-token and per-head context-dependent weighting without requiring additional fusion parameters. The inter-layer behavior is controlled through a single hyperparameter, the sliding window size, which allows smooth adjustment between purely linear and full attention while keeping all layers structurally uniform. Experimental results show that NHA surpasses Transformers and other hybrid baselines on recall-intensive and commonsense reasoning tasks. Furthermore, pretrained LLMs can be structurally hybridized with NHA, achieving competitive accuracy while delivering significant efficiency gains. Code is available at https://github.com/JusenD/NHA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACL 2026, 17 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07019","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:59:54Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:59:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-16T02:18:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06499","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06499","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06499","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhepeng","familyName":"Cen","name":"Cen, Zhepeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haolin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jielin","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Jielin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ding","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Ding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silvio","familyName":"Savarese","name":"Savarese, Silvio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiming","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Caiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiran","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Weiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Webscale-RL: Automated Data Pipeline for Scaling RL Data to Pretraining Levels"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T22:30:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:11:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-10T04:08:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-13T00:22:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success through imitation learning on vast text corpora, but this paradigm creates a training-generation gap and limits robust reasoning. Reinforcement learning (RL) offers a more data-efficient solution capable of bridging this gap, yet its application has been constrained by a critical data bottleneck: existing RL datasets are orders of magnitude smaller and less diverse than web-scale pre-training corpora. To address this, we introduce the Webscale-RL pipeline, a scalable data engine that systematically converts large-scale pre-training documents into millions of diverse, verifiable question-answer pairs for RL. Using this pipeline, we construct the Webscale-RL dataset, containing 1.2 million examples across more than 9 domains. Our experiments show that the model trained on this dataset significantly outperforms continual pretraining and strong data refinement baselines across a suite of benchmarks. Notably, RL training with our dataset proves substantially more efficient, achieving the performance of continual pre-training with up to 100$\\times$ fewer tokens. Our work presents a viable path toward scaling RL to pre-training levels, enabling more capable and efficient language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06499","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:47:41Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:47:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-13T02:08:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07048","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07048","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07048","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntao","familyName":"Gui","name":"Gui, Yuntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Search-R3: Unifying Reasoning and Embedding in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:16:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:50:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-09T10:25:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-10T00:44:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite their remarkable natural language understanding capabilities, Large Language Models (LLMs) have been underutilized for retrieval tasks. We present Search-R3, a novel framework that addresses this limitation by adapting LLMs to generate search embeddings as a direct output of their reasoning process. Our approach exploits LLMs' chain-of-thought capabilities, allowing them to produce more effective embeddings by reasoning step-by-step through complex semantic analyses. We implement this through three complementary mechanisms. (1) a supervised learning stage enables the model's ability to produce quality embeddings, (2) a reinforcement learning (RL) methodology that optimizes embedding generation alongside reasoning, and (3) a specialized RL environment that efficiently handles evolving embedding representations without requiring complete corpus re-encoding at each training iteration. Our extensive evaluations on diverse benchmarks demonstrate that Search-R3 significantly outperforms prior methods by unifying the reasoning and embedding generation processes. This integrated post-training approach represents a substantial advancement in handling complex knowledge-intensive tasks that require both sophisticated reasoning and effective information retrieval. Project page: https://github.com/ytgui/Search-R3"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CHANGELOG: (1) Completed training of Search-R3-Large; (2) Corrected error formulation; (3) Added a `Discussion` section to the appendix. We appreciation to the anonymous reviewers"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07048","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:38Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-10T02:24:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06800","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06800","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06800","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shufan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Shufan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chios","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyang","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hehai","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hehai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heqing","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Heqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Shu","name":"Shu, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengwei","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Chengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FURINA: A Fully Customizable Role-Playing Benchmark via Scalable Multi-Agent Collaboration Pipeline"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:30:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:36:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-12T15:48:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:07:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-06T11:46:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-07T01:24:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) advance in role-playing (RP) tasks, existing benchmarks quickly become obsolete due to their narrow scope, outdated interaction paradigms, and limited adaptability across diverse application scenarios. To address this gap, we introduce FURINA-Builder, a novel multi-agent collaboration pipeline that automatically constructs fully customizable RP benchmarks at any scale. It enables evaluation of arbitrary characters across diverse scenarios and prompt formats, as the first benchmark builder in RP area for adaptable assessment. FURINA-Builder simulates dialogues between a test character and other characters drawn from a well-constructed character-scene pool, while an LLM judge selects fine-grained evaluation dimensions and adjusts the test character's responses into final test utterances. Using this pipeline, we build FURINA-Bench, a new comprehensive role-playing benchmark featuring both established and synthesized test characters, each assessed with dimension-specific evaluation criteria. Human evaluation and preliminary separability analysis justify our pipeline and benchmark design. We conduct extensive evaluations of cutting-edge LLMs and find that o3 and DeepSeek-R1 achieve the best performance on English and Chinese RP tasks, respectively. Across all models, established characters consistently outperform synthesized ones, with reasoning capabilities further amplifying this disparity. Interestingly, we observe that model scale does not monotonically reduce hallucinations. More critically, for reasoning LLMs, we uncover a novel trade-off: reasoning improves RP performance but simultaneously increases RP hallucinations. This trade-off extends to a broader Pareto frontier between RP performance and reliability for all LLMs. These findings demonstrate the effectiveness of FURINA-Builder and the challenge posed by FURINA-Bench."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06800","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:44Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-07T03:26:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06790","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06790","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06790","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tavish","familyName":"McDonald","name":"McDonald, Tavish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stanislav","familyName":"Fort","name":"Fort, Stanislav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhavya","familyName":"Kailkhura","name":"Kailkhura, Bhavya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Bartoldson","name":"Bartoldson, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Get RICH or Die Scaling: Profitably Trading Inference Compute for Robustness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:18:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:35:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-01T18:15:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T02:48:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T04:07:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-27T00:26:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test-time reasoning has raised benchmark performances and even shown promise in addressing the historically intractable problem of making models robust to adversarially out-of-distribution (OOD) data. Indeed, recent work used reasoning to aid satisfaction of model specifications designed to thwart attacks, finding a striking correlation between LLM reasoning effort and robustness to jailbreaks. However, this benefit fades when stronger (e.g. gradient-based or multimodal) attacks are used. This may be expected as models often can't follow instructions on the adversarially OOD data created by such attacks, and instruction following is needed to act in accordance with the attacker-thwarting spec. Thus, we hypothesize that the test-time robustness benefits of specs are unlocked by initial robustness sufficient to follow instructions on OOD data. Namely, we posit the Robustness from Inference Compute Hypothesis (RICH): inference-compute defenses profit as the model's training data better reflects the components of attacked data. Guided by the RICH, we test models of varying initial-robustness levels, finding inference-compute adds robustness even to white-box multimodal attacks, provided the model has sufficient initial robustness. Further evidencing a rich-get-richer dynamic, InternVL 3.5 gpt-oss 20B gains little robustness when its test compute is scaled, but such scaling adds significant robustness if we first robustify its vision encoder (creating the first adversarially robust reasoning VLM in the process). Robustifying models makes attacked components of data more in-distribution (ID), and the RICH suggests this fuels compositional generalization -- understanding OOD data via its ID components -- to following spec instructions on adversarial data. Consistently, we find test-time defenses both build and depend on train-time data and defenses."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06790","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:30Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-27T02:22:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06638","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06638","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06638","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenkang","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Wenkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingtong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xingtong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weicheng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Weicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StaR-KVQA: Structured Reasoning Traces for Implicit-Knowledge Visual Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T04:37:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:23:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-15T09:26:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:29:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-22T14:23:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:09:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge-based Visual Question Answering (KVQA) requires models to ground entities in images and reason over factual knowledge. Recent work has introduced its implicit-knowledge variant, IK-KVQA, where a multimodal large language model (MLLM) is the sole knowledge source and answers are produced without external retrieval. Existing IK-KVQA approaches, however, are typically trained with answer-only supervision: reasoning remains implicit, justifications are often weak or inconsistent, and generalization after standard supervised fine-tuning (SFT) can be brittle. We propose StaR-KVQA, a framework that equips IK-KVQA with dual-path structured reasoning traces - symbolic relation paths over text and vision together with path-grounded natural-language explanations - to provide a stronger inductive bias than generic answer-only supervision. These traces act as modality-aware scaffolds that guide the model toward relevant entities and attributes, offering more structure than generic chain-of-thought supervision while not constraining reasoning to any single fixed path. With a single open-source MLLM, StaR-KVQA constructs and selects traces to build an offline trace-enriched dataset and then performs structure-aware self-distillation; no external retrievers, verifiers, or curated knowledge bases are used, and inference is a single autoregressive pass. Across benchmarks, StaR-KVQA consistently improves both answer accuracy and the transparency of intermediate reasoning, achieving up to +11.3% higher answer accuracy on OK-VQA over the strongest baseline."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8+3+3 pages, code: https://github.com/jianyingzhihe/StaR-KVQA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06638","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:50:57Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:50:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:17:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06261","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06261","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06261","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanke","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zhanke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chentao","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Chentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongze","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zongze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangchao","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Jiangchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weikai","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Weikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianghangfan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianghangfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tangyu","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Tangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linrui","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Linrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brando","familyName":"Miranda","name":"Miranda, Brando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongliang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanmi","familyName":"Koyejo","name":"Koyejo, Sanmi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masashi","familyName":"Sugiyama","name":"Sugiyama, Masashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AlphaApollo: A System for Deep Agentic Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T15:42:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-10T02:38:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-11T00:25:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present AlphaApollo, an agentic reasoning system that targets two bottlenecks in foundation-model reasoning: (1) limited reasoning capacity for complex, long-horizon problem solving and (2) unreliable test-time evolution without trustworthy verification. AlphaApollo orchestrates models and tools via three components: (i) multi-turn agentic reasoning, which formalizes model-environment interaction with structured tool calls and responses; (ii) multi-turn agentic learning, which applies turn-level reinforcement learning to optimize tool-use reasoning while decoupling actions from tool responses for stable training; and (iii) multi-round agentic evolution, which refines solutions through a propose-judge-update loop with tool-assisted verifications and long-horizon memory. Across seven math reasoning benchmarks and multiple model scales, AlphaApollo improves performance through reliable tool use (\u0026gt; 85% tool-call success), substantial gains from multi-turn RL (Avg@32: Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct 1.07% -\u0026gt; 9.64%, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct 8.77% -\u0026gt; 20.35%), and improvements from evolution (e.g., Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct 5.27% -\u0026gt; 7.70%, Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct 16.53% -\u0026gt; 21.08%). This project is still ongoing. We welcome feedback from the community and will frequently update the source code and technical report."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Ongoing project"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06261","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:10Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-11T02:33:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07181","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07181","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07181","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enshen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Enshen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanyu","familyName":"Rong","name":"Rong, Shanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingkun","familyName":"An","name":"An, Jingkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanghang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shanghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TIGeR: Tool-Integrated Geometric Reasoning in Vision-Language Models for Robotics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:20:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:58:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T13:56:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:57:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T07:07:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-05T01:36:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have shown remarkable capabilities in spatial reasoning, yet they remain fundamentally limited to qualitative precision and lack the computational precision required for real-world robotics. Current approaches fail to leverage metric cues from depth sensors and camera calibration, instead reducing geometric problems to pattern recognition tasks that cannot deliver the centimeter-level accuracy essential for robotic manipulation. We present TIGeR (Tool-Integrated Geometric Reasoning), a novel framework that transforms VLMs from perceptual estimators to geometric computers by enabling them to generate and execute precise geometric computations through external tools. Rather than attempting to internalize complex geometric operations within neural networks, TIGeR empowers models to recognize geometric reasoning requirements, synthesize appropriate computational code, and invoke specialized libraries for exact calculations. To support this paradigm, we introduce TIGeR-300K, a comprehensive tool-invocation-oriented dataset covering point transformations, pose estimation, and spatial compatibility verification, complete with tool invocation sequences and intermediate computations. Through a two-stage training pipeline combining supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and reinforcement fine-tuning (RFT) with our proposed hierarchical reward design, TIGeR achieves SOTA performance on geometric reasoning benchmarks while demonstrating centimeter-level precision in real-world robotic manipulation tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07181","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:48Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-05T03:44:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06410","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06410","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06410","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aochong Oliver","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Aochong Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanya","familyName":"Goyal","name":"Goyal, Tanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Off-Trajectory Reasoning: Can LLMs Collaborate on Reasoning Trajectory?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T19:42:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:06:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T23:34:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:13:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning LLMs are trained to verbalize their reasoning process, yielding strong gains on complex tasks. This transparency also opens a promising direction: multiple reasoners can directly collaborate on each other's thinking within a shared trajectory, yielding better inference efficiency and exploration. A key prerequisite, however, is the ability to assess the usefulness and build on another model's partial thinking -- we call this off-trajectory reasoning. Our paper investigates a critical question: can standard solo-reasoning training pipelines deliver desired off-trajectory behaviors? We propose twin tests that capture the two extremes of the off-trajectory spectrum, namely Recoverability, which tests whether LLMs can backtrack from \"distractions\" induced by misleading reasoning traces, and Guidability, which tests their ability to build upon correct reasoning from stronger collaborators. Our study evaluates 15 open-weight LLMs (1.5B-32B) and reveals a counterintuitive finding -- \"stronger\" LLMs on benchmarks are often more fragile under distraction. Moreover, all models tested fail to effectively leverage guiding steps from collaborators on problems beyond their inherent capabilities with solve rates remaining under 9.2%. Finally, we conduct control studies to isolate the effects of three factors in post-training on these behaviors: the choice of distillation teacher, the use of RL, and data selection strategy. Our results provide actionable insights for training natively strong reasoning collaborators; e.g., we find that suboptimal recoverability behaviors of teacher models are transferred to distilled students even if the distillation trajectories are correct. Taken together, this work lays the groundwork for evaluating multi-model collaborations in shared reasoning trajectories and highlights the limitations of off-the-shelf reasoning LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06410","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:45:38Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:45:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:18:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07233","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07233","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07233","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhivar","familyName":"Sourati","name":"Sourati, Zhivar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marianne Menglin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Marianne Menglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yazhe","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yazhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengqing","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Mengqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujeeth","familyName":"Bharadwaj","name":"Bharadwaj, Sujeeth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyu","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Kyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujith","familyName":"Ravi","name":"Ravi, Sujith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Morteza","familyName":"Dehghani","name":"Dehghani, Morteza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Roth","name":"Roth, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LAD-RAG: Layout-aware Dynamic RAG for Visually-Rich Document Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:02:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:00:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-28T00:40:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:19:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Question answering over visually rich documents (VRDs) requires reasoning not only over isolated content but also over documents' structural organization and cross-page dependencies. However, conventional retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) methods encode content in isolated chunks during ingestion, losing structural and cross-page dependencies, and retrieve a fixed number of pages at inference, regardless of the specific demands of the question or context. This often results in incomplete evidence retrieval and degraded answer quality for multi-page reasoning tasks. To address these limitations, we propose LAD-RAG, a novel Layout-Aware Dynamic RAG framework. During ingestion, LAD-RAG constructs a symbolic document graph that captures layout structure and cross-page dependencies, adding it alongside standard neural embeddings to yield a more holistic representation of the document. During inference, an LLM agent dynamically interacts with the neural and symbolic indices to adaptively retrieve the necessary evidence based on the query. Experiments on MMLongBench-Doc, LongDocURL, DUDE, and MP-DocVQA demonstrate that LAD-RAG improves retrieval, achieving over 90% perfect recall on average without any top-k tuning, and outperforming baseline retrievers by up to 20% in recall at comparable noise levels, yielding higher QA accuracy with minimal latency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07233","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:02Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06823","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06823","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06823","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riku","familyName":"Mochizuki","name":"Mochizuki, Riku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shusuke","familyName":"Komatsu","name":"Komatsu, Shusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Souta","familyName":"Noguchi","name":"Noguchi, Souta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuto","familyName":"Ataka","name":"Ataka, Kazuto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exposing Citation Vulnerabilities in Generative Engines"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:47:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:37:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T03:28:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:36:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We analyze answers generated by generative engines (GEs) from the perspectives of citation publishers and the content-injection barrier, defined as the difficulty for attackers to manipulate answers to user prompts by placing malicious content on the web. GEs integrate two functions: web search and answer generation that cites web pages using large language models. Because anyone can publish information on the web, GEs are vulnerable to poisoning attacks. Existing studies of citation evaluation focus on how faithfully answer content reflects cited sources, leaving unexamined which web sources should be selected as citations to defend against poisoning attacks. To fill this gap, we introduce evaluation criteria that assess poisoning threats using the citation information contained in answers. Our criteria classify the publisher attributes of citations to estimate the content-injection barrier thereby revealing the threat of poisoning attacks in current GEs. We conduct experiments in political domains in Japan and the United States (U.S.) using our criteria and show that citations from official party websites (primary sources) are approximately \\(25\\%\\)--\\(45\\%\\) in the U.S. and \\(60\\%\\)--\\(65\\%\\) in Japan, indicating that U.S. political answers are at higher risk of poisoning attacks. We also find that sources with low content-injection barriers are frequently cited yet are poorly reflected in answer content. To mitigate this threat, we discuss how publishers of primary sources can increase exposure of their web content in answers and show that well-known techniques are limited by language differences."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, under-reviewing at a conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06823","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:16Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06377","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06377","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06377","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishabh","familyName":"Ranjan","name":"Ranjan, Rishabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valter","familyName":"Hudovernik","name":"Hudovernik, Valter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Znidar","name":"Znidar, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charilaos","familyName":"Kanatsoulis","name":"Kanatsoulis, Charilaos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roshan","familyName":"Upendra","name":"Upendra, Roshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahmoud","familyName":"Mohammadi","name":"Mohammadi, Mahmoud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joe","familyName":"Meyer","name":"Meyer, Joe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tom","familyName":"Palczewski","name":"Palczewski, Tom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlos","familyName":"Guestrin","name":"Guestrin, Carlos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jure","familyName":"Leskovec","name":"Leskovec, Jure","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Relational Transformer: Toward Zero-Shot Foundation Models for Relational Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:51:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:04:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-22T22:13:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-24T00:10:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T07:22:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:45:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pretrained transformers readily adapt to new sequence modeling tasks via zero-shot prompting, but relational domains still lack architectures that transfer across datasets and tasks. The core challenge is the diversity of relational data, with varying heterogeneous schemas, graph structures and functional dependencies. In this paper, we present the Relational Transformer (RT) architecture, which can be pretrained on diverse relational databases and directly applied to unseen datasets and tasks without task- or dataset-specific fine-tuning, or retrieval of in-context examples. RT (i) incorporates task specification via task table prompting, (ii) tokenizes cells with table/column metadata, (iii) is pretrained via masked token prediction, and (iv) utilizes a novel Relational Attention mechanism over columns, rows, and primary-foreign key links. Pretrained on RelBench datasets spanning tasks such as churn and sales forecasting, RT attains strong zero-shot performance, averaging 93% of fully supervised AUROC on binary classification tasks with a single forward pass of a 22M parameter model, as opposed to 84% for a 27B LLM. Fine-tuning yields state-of-the-art results with high sample efficiency. Our experimental analyses show that RT's zero-shot transfer leverages task context, relational attention patterns and schema semantics. Overall, RT provides a practical path toward foundation models for relational data. Code, models, data: https://github.com/snap-stanford/relational-transformer."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06377","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06292","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06292","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06292","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yike","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ChainMPQ: Interleaved Text-Image Reasoning Chains for Mitigating Relation Hallucinations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:19:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-01T01:39:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:56:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) achieve strong performance in multimodal tasks, hallucinations continue to hinder their reliability. Among the three categories of hallucinations, which include object, attribute, and relation, relation hallucinations account for the largest proportion but have received the least attention. To address this issue, we propose ChainMPQ (Multi-Perspective Questions guided Interleaved Chain of Image and Text), a training-free method that improves relational inference in LVLMs by utilizing accumulated textual and visual memories. ChainMPQ first extracts subject and object keywords from the question to enhance the corresponding image regions. It then constructs multi-perspective questions that focus on the three core components of a relationship: the subject, the object, and the relation that links them. These questions are sequentially input to the model, with textual and visual memories from earlier steps providing supporting context for subsequent ones, thereby forming an interleaved chain of images and text that guides progressive relational reasoning. Experiments on multiple LVLMs and benchmarks show that ChainMPQ substantially reduces relation hallucinations, while ablation studies further validate the effectiveness of its three core modules."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06292","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07172","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07172","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07172","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kelvin Kiu-Wai","familyName":"Tam","name":"Tam, Kelvin Kiu-Wai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Newt Hue-Nam K.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Newt Hue-Nam K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baixuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Baixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaowei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayang","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jiayang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong Ting","familyName":"Tsang","name":"Tsang, Hong Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianqing","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Tianqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangqiu","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yangqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ginny Y.","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Ginny Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"See","name":"See, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NewtonBench: Benchmarking Generalizable Scientific Law Discovery in LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:12:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:57:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-09T09:13:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-10T01:33:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T08:11:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:30:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are emerging as powerful tools for scientific law discovery, a foundational challenge in AI-driven science. However, existing benchmarks for this task suffer from a fundamental methodological trilemma, forcing a trade-off between scientific relevance, scalability, and resistance to memorization. Furthermore, they oversimplify discovery as static function fitting, failing to capture the authentic scientific process of uncovering embedded laws through the interactive exploration of complex model systems. To address these critical gaps, we introduce NewtonBench, a benchmark comprising 324 scientific law discovery tasks across 12 physics domains. Our design mitigates the evaluation trilemma by using counterfactual law shifts - systematic alterations of canonical laws - to generate a vast suite of problems that are scalable, scientifically relevant, and memorization-resistant. Moreover, we elevate the evaluation from static function fitting to interactive model discovery, requiring agents to experimentally probe simulated complex systems to uncover hidden principles. Our extensive experiment reveals a clear but fragile capability for discovery in frontier LLMs: this ability degrades precipitously with increasing system complexity and exhibits extreme sensitivity to observational noise. Notably, we uncover a paradoxical effect of tool assistance: providing a code interpreter can hinder more capable models by inducing a premature shift from exploration to exploitation, causing them to satisfice on suboptimal solutions. These results demonstrate that robust, generalizable discovery in complex, interactive environments remains the core challenge. By providing a scalable, robust, and scientifically authentic testbed, NewtonBench offers a crucial tool for measuring true progress and guiding the development of next-generation AI agents capable of genuine scientific discovery."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07172","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:35Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T03:09:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06924","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06924","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06924","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jordan","familyName":"Nelson","name":"Nelson, Jordan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Almas","familyName":"Baimagambetov","name":"Baimagambetov, Almas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantinos","familyName":"Avgerinakis","name":"Avgerinakis, Konstantinos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolaos","familyName":"Polatidis","name":"Polatidis, Nikolaos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ethical AI prompt recommendations in large language models using collaborative filtering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T12:03:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-27T01:48:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1080/17445760.2025.2573086","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) shape AI development, ensuring ethical prompt recommendations is crucial. LLMs offer innovation but risk bias, fairness issues, and accountability concerns. Traditional oversight methods struggle with scalability, necessitating dynamic solutions. This paper proposes using collaborative filtering, a technique from recommendation systems, to enhance ethical prompt selection. By leveraging user interactions, it promotes ethical guidelines while reducing bias. Contributions include a synthetic dataset for prompt recommendations and the application of collaborative filtering. The work also tackles challenges in ethical AI, such as bias mitigation, transparency, and preventing unethical prompt engineering."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted to by the International Journal of Parallel, Emergent \u0026amp; Distributed Systems (Taylor and Francis) and has an assigned DOI. We have already chose to make this open access using CC BY. The article is not yet available online on the publisher's website. The DOI is: doi.org/10.1080/17445760.2025.2573086"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06924","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:57:38Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:57:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:36:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07238","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07238","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07238","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xunyi","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Xunyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingyi","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Dingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"McAuley","name":"McAuley, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Benchmarks Age: Temporal Misalignment through Large Language Model Factuality Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:06:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:01:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-20T17:39:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T03:30:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid evolution of large language models (LLMs) and the real world has outpaced the static nature of widely used evaluation benchmarks, raising concerns about their reliability for evaluating LLM factuality. While substantial works continue to rely on the popular but old benchmarks, their temporal misalignment with real-world facts and modern LLMs, and their effects on LLM factuality evaluation remain underexplored. Therefore, in this work, we present a systematic investigation of this issue by examining five popular factuality benchmarks and eight LLMs released across different years. An up-to-date fact retrieval pipeline and three metrics are tailored to quantify benchmark aging and its impact on LLM factuality evaluation. Experimental results and analysis illustrate that a considerable portion of samples in the widely used factuality benchmarks are outdated, leading to unreliable assessments of LLM factuality. We hope our work can provide a testbed to assess the reliability of a benchmark for LLM factuality evaluation and inspire more research on the benchmark aging issue. Codes are available in https://github.com/JiangXunyi/BenchAge."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EACL 2026 Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07238","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:09Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06243","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06243","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06243","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qihua","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Qihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis","familyName":"Figueroa","name":"Figueroa, Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Handong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Handong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kushal","familyName":"Kafle","name":"Kafle, Kushal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Kuen","name":"Kuen, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihong","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Zhihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Scott","familyName":"Cohen","name":"Cohen, Scott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CoT Referring: Improving Referring Expression Tasks with Grounded Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T08:50:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-17T09:14:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T01:23:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Referring Expression Comprehension and Segmentation are critical tasks for assessing the integration of language understanding and image comprehension, serving as benchmarks for Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) capabilities. To address these challenges, we propose a new strategy, CoT Referring, which enhances model reasoning across modalities through a structured, chain-of-thought training data structure. Our approach systematically parses textual structures to a sequential referring step, where in each step it identifies relationships and ensures consistent reference alignment, thereby improving accuracy in complex query scenarios. We restructure the training data to enforce a new output form, providing new annotations for existing datasets and compiling an evaluation benchmark from existing resources. This benchmark is designed explicitly for complex referring cases. We also integrate detection and segmentation capabilities into a unified MLLM framework, training it with a novel adaptive weighted loss to optimize performance. Experimental results on our curated benchmark and RefCOCO/+/g demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach, with a notable increase of 2.5%+ over baseline models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"MLLM, Referring Expression Segmentation"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06243","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:45Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06534","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06534","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06534","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahe","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Jiahe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhijay","familyName":"Paladugu","name":"Paladugu, Abhijay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyan","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Chenyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beneficial Reasoning Behaviors in Agentic Search and Effective Post-training to Obtain Them"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T00:20:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:14:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-22T06:56:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T01:23:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-16T18:30:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-19T01:49:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agentic search requires large language models (LLMs) to perform multi-step search to solve complex information-seeking tasks, imposing unique challenges on their reasoning capabilities. However, what constitutes effective reasoning for agentic search and how it can be learned remains unclear. In this work, we first investigate the reasoning behaviors that enable success in agentic search. By comparing successful and failed trajectories via an LLM-based analysis pipeline, we identify four beneficial behaviors: Information Verification, Authority Evaluation, Adaptive Search, and Error Recovery. Building on this, we propose Behavior Priming, a training approach that equips agentic search models with these reasoning behaviors before reinforcement learning (RL). Specifically, it first performs supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on collected trajectories exhibiting the identified behaviors to cultivate these behaviors, and then applies standard RL to further improve task performance. Experiments on Qwen3-1.7B and Llama3.2-3B-Instruct show that Behavior Priming yields relative improvements over direct RL by 37.2\\% on three web benchmarks and 6.2\\% on seven multi-hop QA benchmarks, and outperforms the SFT-then-RL baseline using outcome-correct trajectories for fine-tuning. Crucially, we show that these reasoning behaviors matter more than outcome correctness in the priming stage prior to RL. Further analysis reveals that Behavior Priming enhances exploration (pass@8) and test-time scaling (search step number), providing a robust foundation for RL. Our code are avalible at https://github.com/cxcscmu/Behavior-Priming-for-Agentic-Search."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06534","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:48:31Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:48:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-19T02:45:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07309","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07309","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07309","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Derek","familyName":"Hommel","name":"Hommel, Derek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusuf Denizay","familyName":"Dönder","name":"Dönder, Yusuf Denizay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungyong","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Sungyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Mimno","name":"Mimno, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Unso Eun Seo","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Unso Eun Seo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agent Bain vs. Agent McKinsey: A New Text-to-SQL Benchmark for the Business Domain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:57:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:04:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T02:27:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:21:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-11T00:42:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:37:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-13T22:44:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:08:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-SQL benchmarks have traditionally only tested simple data access as a translation task of natural language to SQL queries. But in reality, users tend to ask diverse questions that require more complex responses including data-driven predictions or recommendations. Using the business domain as a motivating example, we introduce CORGI, a new benchmark that expands text-to-SQL to reflect practical database queries encountered by end users. CORGI is composed of synthetic databases inspired by enterprises such as DoorDash, Airbnb, and Lululemon. It provides questions across four increasingly complicated categories of business queries: descriptive, explanatory, predictive, and recommendational. This challenge calls for causal reasoning, temporal forecasting, and strategic recommendation, reflecting multi-level and multi-step agentic intelligence. We find that LLM performance degrades on higher-level questions as question complexity increases. CORGI also introduces and encourages the text-to-SQL community to consider new automatic methods for evaluating open-ended, qualitative responses in data access tasks. Our experiments show that LLMs exhibit an average 33.12% lower success execution rate (SER) on CORGI compared to existing benchmarks such as BIRD, highlighting the substantially higher complexity of real-world business needs. We release the CORGI dataset, an evaluation framework, and a submission website to support future research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, under review for ACL ARR"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07309","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:51Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-15T03:10:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07038","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07038","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07038","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wenxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuanyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guhan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linyue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Linyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hongyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tool-Augmented Policy Optimization: Synergizing Reasoning and Adaptive Tool Use with Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:04:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:49:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-12T08:14:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T02:09:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have popularized test-time scaling, where models generate additional reasoning tokens before producing final answers. These approaches have demonstrated significant performance improvements on benchmarks involving mathematical reasoning. However, language models relying solely on direct inference still struggle with tasks demanding up-to-date knowledge or computational tools such as calculators and code interpreters for complex arithmetic operations. To overcome these limitations, we propose Tool-Augmented Policy Optimization (TAPO), a novel reinforcement learning framework that systematically integrates multi-hop reasoning with adaptive tool-calling capabilities. Our approach employs a modified version of Dynamic Sampling Policy Optimization (DAPO), a recently developed RL paradigm, which we adapt specifically for tool invocation scenarios, enabling models to dynamically interleave complex reasoning with on-demand tool usage (including search APIs and Python interpreters).\n To support this research, we introduce two new datasets: TAPO-easy-60K and TAPO-hard-18K, specifically designed to train and evaluate both fact-based reasoning and mathematical calculation capabilities. Our experiments on Qwen2.5-3B and Qwen2.5-7B models demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach, with both models achieving state-of-the-art performance on tasks requiring external knowledge and mathematical computation among methods with comparable parameters. Notably, TAPO achieves more efficient tool utilization than baseline methods while preventing excessive calls caused by reward hacking. These results highlight the significant potential of combining advanced reasoning with tool usage to enhance model performance in knowledge-intensive and computationally demanding tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07038","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:22Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:27:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07318","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07318","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07318","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhao","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yunhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghao","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Qinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuehan","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Xuehan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lai","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Lai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Artificial Hippocampus Networks for Efficient Long-Context Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:59:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:04:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-17T07:08:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-18T01:25:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-sequence modeling faces a fundamental trade-off between the efficiency of compressive fixed-size memory in RNN-like models and the fidelity of lossless growing memory in attention-based Transformers. Inspired by the Multi-Store Model in cognitive science, we introduce a memory framework of artificial neural networks. Our method maintains a sliding window of the Transformer's KV cache as lossless short-term memory, while a learnable module termed Artificial Hippocampus Network (AHN) recurrently compresses out-of-window information into a fixed-size compact long-term memory. To validate this framework, we instantiate AHNs using modern RNN-like architectures, including Mamba2, DeltaNet, and GatedDeltaNet to augment open-weight LLMs. We also propose an efficient self-distillation training method where the base model's all parameters are frozen and only the parameters from AHNs are optimized. For inference, our method sets a default large sliding window size of 32k for attention, and AHNs activate only when the sequence length exceeds the 32k window, addressing the quadratic-complexity issue of attention that emerges at that scale. Extensive experiments on long-context benchmarks LV-Eval and InfiniteBench demonstrate that AHN-augmented models consistently outperform sliding window baselines and achieve performance comparable or even superior to full-attention models, while substantially reducing computational and memory requirements. For instance, augmenting the Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct with AHNs reduces inference FLOPs by 40.5% and memory cache by 74.0%, while improving its average score on LV-Eval (128k sequence length) from 4.41 to 5.88. Code is available at: https://github.com/ByteDance-Seed/AHN."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code: https://github.com/ByteDance-Seed/AHN"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07318","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:07:04Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:07:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-18T03:07:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06349","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06349","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06349","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moein E.","familyName":"Samadi","name":"Samadi, Moein E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Schuppert","name":"Schuppert, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Flexible Swarm Learning May Outpace Foundation Models in Essential Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:10:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:03:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-30T11:47:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:45:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models have rapidly advanced AI, raising the question of whether their decisions will ultimately surpass human strategies in real-world domains. The exponential, and possibly super-exponential, pace of AI development makes such analysis elusive. Nevertheless, many application areas that matter for daily life and society show only modest gains so far; a prominent case is diagnosing and treating dynamically evolving disease in intensive care.\n The common challenge is adapting complex systems to dynamic environments. Effective strategies must optimize outcomes in systems composed of strongly interacting functions while avoiding shared side effects; this requires reliable, self-adaptive modeling. These tasks align with building digital twins of highly complex systems whose mechanisms are not fully or quantitatively understood. It is therefore essential to develop methods for self-adapting AI models with minimal data and limited mechanistic knowledge. As this challenge extends beyond medicine, AI should demonstrate clear superiority in these settings before assuming broader decision-making roles.\n We identify the curse of dimensionality as a fundamental barrier to efficient self-adaptation and argue that monolithic foundation models face conceptual limits in overcoming it. As an alternative, we propose a decentralized architecture of interacting small agent networks (SANs). We focus on agents representing the specialized substructure of the system, where each agent covers only a subset of the full system functions. Drawing on mathematical results on the learning behavior of SANs and evidence from existing applications, we argue that swarm-learning in diverse swarms can enable self-adaptive SANs to deliver superior decision-making in dynamic environments compared with monolithic foundation models, though at the cost of reduced reproducibility in detail."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06349","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:14Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:47:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06251","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06251","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06251","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ieva","familyName":"Bagdonaviciute","name":"Bagdonaviciute, Ieva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vibhav","familyName":"Vineet","name":"Vineet, Vibhav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Physics Knowledge in Frontier Models: A Diagnostic Study of Failure Modes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T22:30:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-14T21:33:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:11:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While recent Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have achieved impressive progress, it remains difficult to determine why they succeed or fail on complex reasoning tasks. Traditional benchmarks evaluate what models can answer correctly, not why they succeed or fail. In this work, we perform a failure-mode analysis of six frontier VLMs on three physics-based benchmarks - Physion, Physion++, and CLEVRER - by introducing custom subtests (for Physion and Physion++) and an integration of existing benchmark categories (for CLEVRER) to factor benchmark performance into distinct, testable capabilities. These subtests isolate perception (object, color, and occlusion recognition) and physics understanding (motion prediction and spatial reasoning), enabling us to test whether models attend to the correct entities and dynamics underlying their answers. Counterintuitively, subtest mastery correlates only weakly with benchmark accuracy: models often answer correctly without grounding in perception or physics. This suggests that current VLMs sometimes achieve benchmark scores for the wrong reasons, underscoring the need for diagnostics that expose hidden failure modes beyond aggregate metrics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 7 figures. Preprint. v2: Updated experiments and diagnostics; formatting fixes"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06251","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:56Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:16:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06557","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06557","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06557","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milad","familyName":"Aghajohari","name":"Aghajohari, Milad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kamran","familyName":"Chitsaz","name":"Chitsaz, Kamran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirhossein","familyName":"Kazemnejad","name":"Kazemnejad, Amirhossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarath","familyName":"Chandar","name":"Chandar, Sarath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Sordoni","name":"Sordoni, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Courville","name":"Courville, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siva","familyName":"Reddy","name":"Reddy, Siva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Markovian Thinker: Architecture-Agnostic Linear Scaling of Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T01:18:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:16:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-10T03:21:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-11T02:12:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-13T02:49:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-14T01:16:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has recently become a strong recipe for training reasoning LLMs that produce long chains of thought (LongCoT). Yet the standard RL \"thinking environment\", where the state is the prompt plus all prior reasoning tokens, makes the state unbounded and forces attention-based policies to pay quadratic compute as thoughts lengthen. We revisit the environment itself. We propose Markovian Thinking, a paradigm in which the policy advances reasoning while conditioning on a constant-size state, decoupling thinking length from context size. As an immediate consequence this yields linear compute with constant memory. We instantiate this idea with Delethink, an RL environment that structures reasoning into fixed-size chunks. Within each chunk, the model thinks as usual; at the boundary, the environment resets the context and reinitializes the prompt with a short carryover. Through RL, the policy learns to write a textual state near the end of each chunk sufficient for seamless continuation of reasoning after reset. Trained in this environment, an R1-Distill 1.5B model reasons in 8K-token chunks yet thinks up to 24K tokens, matching or surpassing LongCoT-RL trained with a 24K budget. With test-time scaling, Delethink continues to improve where LongCoT plateaus. The effect of linear compute is substantial: we empirically estimate at 96K average thinking length LongCoT-RL costs 27 H100-months vs. 7 for Delethink. Analysis at RL initialization shows off-the-shelf reasoning models (1.5B-120B) often sample Markovian traces zero-shot across diverse benchmarks, providing positive samples that make RL effective at scale. Our results show that redesigning the thinking environment is a powerful lever: it enables very long reasoning without quadratic overhead and opens a path toward efficient, scalable reasoning LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06557","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:49:03Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:49:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-14T03:11:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06915","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06915","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06915","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zecheng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Zecheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baibei","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Baibei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quantong","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Quantong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haitian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haitian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobo","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Xiaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juntao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Juntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LongRM: Revealing and Unlocking the Context Boundary of Reward Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T11:48:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:42:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T05:29:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-05T01:24:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reward model (RM) plays a pivotal role in aligning large language model (LLM) with human preferences. As real-world applications increasingly involve long history trajectories, e.g., LLM agent, it becomes indispensable to evaluate whether a model's responses are not only high-quality but also grounded in and consistent with the provided context. Yet, current RMs remain confined to short-context settings and primarily focus on response-level attributes (e.g., safety or helpfulness), while largely neglecting the critical dimension of long context-response consistency. In this work, we introduce Long-RewardBench, a benchmark specifically designed for long-context RM evaluation, featuring both Pairwise Comparison and Best-of-N tasks. Our preliminary study reveals that even state-of-the-art generative RMs exhibit significant fragility in long-context scenarios, failing to maintain context-aware preference judgments. Motivated by the analysis of failure patterns observed in model outputs, we propose a general multi-stage training strategy that effectively scales arbitrary models into robust Long-context RMs (LongRMs). Experiments show that our approach not only substantially improves performance on long-context evaluation but also preserves strong short-context capability. Notably, our 8B LongRM outperforms much larger 70B-scale baselines and matches the performance of the proprietary Gemini 2.5 Pro model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06915","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:57:26Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:57:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-05T03:12:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06658","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06658","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06658","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaman","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jiaman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zikang","familyName":"Leng","name":"Leng, Zikang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dana","familyName":"McKay","name":"McKay, Dana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Damiano","familyName":"Spina","name":"Spina, Damiano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johanne R.","familyName":"Trippas","name":"Trippas, Johanne R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can We Hide Machines in the Crowd? Quantifying Equivalence in LLM-in-the-loop Annotation Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:17:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:23:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-29T03:24:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:23:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3767695.3769508","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many evaluations of large language models (LLMs) in text annotation focus primarily on the correctness of the output, typically comparing model-generated labels to human-annotated ``ground truth'' using standard performance metrics. In contrast, our study moves beyond effectiveness alone. We aim to explore how labeling decisions -- by both humans and LLMs -- can be statistically evaluated across individuals. Rather than treating LLMs purely as annotation systems, we approach LLMs as an alternative annotation mechanism that may be capable of mimicking the subjective judgments made by humans. To assess this, we develop a statistical evaluation method based on Krippendorff's $α$, paired bootstrapping, and the Two One-Sided t-Tests (TOST) equivalence test procedure. This evaluation method tests whether an LLM can blend into a group of human annotators without being distinguishable.\n We apply this approach to two datasets -- MovieLens 100K and PolitiFact -- and find that the LLM is statistically indistinguishable from a human annotator in the former ($p = 0.004$), but not in the latter ($p = 0.155$), highlighting task-dependent differences. It also enables early evaluation on a small sample of human data to inform whether LLMs are suitable for large-scale annotation in a given application."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at SIGIR-AP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06658","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:25Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-30T02:18:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07230","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07230","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07230","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yimeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yimeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dakuo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dakuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Customer-R1: Personalized Simulation of Human Behaviors via RL-based LLM Agent in Online Shopping"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:00:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:00:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-18T04:00:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:19:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Simulating step-wise human behavior with Large Language Models (LLMs) has become an emerging research direction, enabling applications in various practical domains. While prior methods, including prompting, supervised fine-tuning (SFT), and reinforcement learning (RL), have shown promise in modeling step-wise behavior, they primarily learn a population-level policy without conditioning on a user's persona, yielding generic rather than personalized simulations. In this work, we pose a critical question: how can LLM agents better simulate personalized user behavior? We introduce Customer-R1, an RL-based method for personalized, step-wise user behavior simulation in online shopping environments. Our policy is conditioned on an explicit persona, and we optimize next-step rationale and action generation via action correctness reward signals. Experiments on the OPeRA dataset emonstrate that Customer-R1 not only significantly outperforms prompting and SFT-based baselines in next-action prediction tasks, but also better matches users' action distribution, indicating higher fidelity in personalized behavior simulation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07230","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:04:57Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:04:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:42:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06917","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06917","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06917","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng-Han","familyName":"Chiang","name":"Chiang, Cheng-Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaofei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linjie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Linjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chung-Ching","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Chung-Ching","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujie","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhendong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhendong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhengyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hung-yi","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hung-yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lijuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SHANKS: Simultaneous Hearing and Thinking for Spoken Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T11:48:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:42:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-18T07:57:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:24:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current large language models (LLMs) and spoken language models (SLMs) begin thinking and taking actions only after the user has finished their turn. This prevents the model from interacting during the user's turn and can lead to high response latency while it waits to think. Consequently, thinking after receiving the full input is not suitable for speech-to-speech interaction, where real-time, low-latency exchange is important. We address this by noting that humans naturally \"think while listening.\" In this paper, we propose SHANKS, a general inference framework that enables SLMs to generate unspoken chain-of-thought reasoning while listening to the user input. SHANKS streams the input speech in fixed-duration chunks and, as soon as a chunk is received, generates unspoken reasoning based on all previous speech and reasoning, while the user continues speaking. SHANKS uses this unspoken reasoning to decide whether to interrupt the user and to make tool calls to complete the task. We demonstrate that SHANKS enhances real-time user-SLM interaction in two scenarios: (1) when the user is presenting a step-by-step solution to a math problem, SHANKS can listen, reason, and interrupt when the user makes a mistake, achieving 37.1% higher interruption accuracy than a baseline that interrupts without thinking; and (2) in a tool-augmented dialogue, SHANKS can complete 56.9% of the tool calls before the user finishes their turn. Overall, SHANKS moves toward models that keep thinking throughout the conversation, not only after a turn ends. Animated illustrations of Shanks can be found at https://d223302.github.io/SHANKS/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Work in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06917","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:57:29Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:57:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:42:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07242","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07242","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07242","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leitian","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Leitian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilia","familyName":"Kulikov","name":"Kulikov, Ilia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Swarnadeep","familyName":"Saha","name":"Saha, Swarnadeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianlu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tianlu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharon","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sharon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason E","familyName":"Weston","name":"Weston, Jason E","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid Reinforcement: When Reward Is Sparse, It's Better to Be Dense"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:09:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:01:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T17:01:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:09:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-17T10:03:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-20T00:37:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Post-training for reasoning of large language models (LLMs) increasingly relies on verifiable rewards: deterministic checkers that provide 0-1 correctness signals. While reliable, such binary feedback is brittle--many tasks admit partially correct or alternative answers that verifiers under-credit, and the resulting all-or-nothing supervision limits learning. Reward models offer richer, continuous feedback, which can serve as a complementary supervisory signal to verifiers. We introduce HERO (Hybrid Ensemble Reward Optimization), a reinforcement learning framework that integrates verifier signals with reward-model scores in a structured way. HERO employs stratified normalization to bound reward-model scores within verifier-defined groups, preserving correctness while refining quality distinctions, and variance-aware weighting to emphasize challenging prompts where dense signals matter most. Across diverse mathematical reasoning benchmarks, HERO consistently outperforms RM-only and verifier-only baselines, with strong gains on both verifiable and hard-to-verify tasks. Our results show that hybrid reward design retains the stability of verifiers while leveraging the nuance of reward models to advance reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07242","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:14Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-20T02:02:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07312","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07312","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07312","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumeet Ramesh","familyName":"Motwani","name":"Motwani, Sumeet Ramesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alesia","familyName":"Ivanova","name":"Ivanova, Alesia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Ziyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip","familyName":"Torr","name":"Torr, Philip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riashat","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, Riashat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shital","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Shital","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian Schroeder","familyName":"de Witt","name":"de Witt, Christian Schroeder","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charles","familyName":"London","name":"London, Charles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"h1: Bootstrapping LLMs to Reason over Longer Horizons via Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:58:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:04:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T17:57:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T01:07:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models excel at short-horizon reasoning tasks, but performance drops as reasoning horizon lengths increase. Existing approaches to combat this rely on inference-time scaffolding or costly step-level supervision, neither of which scales easily. In this work, we introduce a scalable method to bootstrap long-horizon reasoning capabilities using only existing, abundant short-horizon data. Our approach synthetically composes simple problems into complex, multi-step dependency chains of arbitrary length. We train models on this data using outcome-only rewards under a curriculum that automatically increases in complexity, allowing RL training to be scaled much further without saturating. Empirically, our method generalizes remarkably well: curriculum training on composed 6th-grade level math problems (GSM8K) boosts accuracy on longer, competition-level benchmarks (GSM-Symbolic, MATH-500, AIME) by up to 2.06x. It also transfers significantly to diverse out-of-distribution ReasoningGym domains and long-context benchmarks, indicating broader generalization. Importantly, our long-horizon improvements are significantly higher than baselines even at high pass@k, showing that models can learn new reasoning paths under RL. Theoretically, we show that curriculum RL with outcome rewards achieves an exponential improvement in sample complexity over full-horizon training, providing training signal comparable to dense supervision. h1 therefore introduces an efficient path towards scaling RL for long-horizon problems using only existing data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint, 31 pages, 8 figures, long-horizon reasoning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07312","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:55Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-16T02:26:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06343","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06343","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06343","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fikret Mert","familyName":"Gultekin","name":"Gultekin, Fikret Mert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oscar","familyName":"Lilja","name":"Lilja, Oscar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ranim","familyName":"Khojah","name":"Khojah, Ranim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rebekka","familyName":"Wohlrab","name":"Wohlrab, Rebekka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marvin","familyName":"Damschen","name":"Damschen, Marvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mazen","familyName":"Mohamad","name":"Mohamad, Mazen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Leveraging Large Language Models for Cybersecurity Risk Assessment -- A Case from Forestry Cyber-Physical Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:07:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:02:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T19:52:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:46:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In safety-critical software systems, cybersecurity activities become essential, with risk assessment being one of the most critical. In many software teams, cybersecurity experts are either entirely absent or represented by only a small number of specialists. As a result, the workload for these experts becomes high, and software engineers would need to conduct cybersecurity activities themselves. This creates a need for a tool to support cybersecurity experts and engineers in evaluating vulnerabilities and threats during the risk assessment process. This paper explores the potential of leveraging locally hosted large language models (LLMs) with retrieval-augmented generation to support cybersecurity risk assessment in the forestry domain while complying with data protection and privacy requirements that limit external data sharing. We performed a design science study involving 12 experts in interviews, interactive sessions, and a survey within a large-scale project. The results demonstrate that LLMs can assist cybersecurity experts by generating initial risk assessments, identifying threats, and providing redundancy checks. The results also highlight the necessity for human oversight to ensure accuracy and compliance. Despite trust concerns, experts were willing to utilize LLMs in specific evaluation and assistance roles, rather than solely relying on their generative capabilities. This study provides insights that encourage the use of LLM-based agents to support the risk assessment process of cyber-physical systems in safety-critical domains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at Autonomous Agents in Software Engineering (AgenticSE) Workshop, co-located with ASE 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06343","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:06Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:24:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07284","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07284","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07284","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"MohammadHossein","familyName":"Rezaei","name":"Rezaei, MohammadHossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Vacareanu","name":"Vacareanu, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clinton","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Clinton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunzhong","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yunzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Afra Feyza","familyName":"Akyürek","name":"Akyürek, Afra Feyza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Online Rubrics Elicitation from Pairwise Comparisons"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:44:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:03:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T18:26:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:04:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Rubrics provide a flexible way to train LLMs on open-ended long-form answers where verifiable rewards are not applicable and human preferences provide coarse signals. Prior work shows that reinforcement learning with rubric-based rewards leads to consistent gains in LLM post-training. Most existing approaches rely on rubrics that remain static over the course of training. Such static rubrics, however, are vulnerable to reward-hacking type behaviors and fail to capture emergent desiderata that arise during training. We introduce Online Rubrics Elicitation (OnlineRubrics), a method that dynamically curates evaluation criteria in an online manner through pairwise comparisons of responses from current and reference policies. This online process enables continuous identification and mitigation of errors as training proceeds. Empirically, this approach yields consistent improvements of up to 8% over training exclusively with static rubrics across AlpacaEval, GPQA, ArenaHard as well as the validation sets of expert questions and rubrics. We qualitatively analyze the elicited criteria and identify prominent themes such as transparency, practicality, organization, and reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07284","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:14Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:18:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06509","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06509","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06509","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shih-Yao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Shih-Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sibendu","familyName":"Paul","name":"Paul, Sibendu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caren","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Caren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Captions to Keyframes: KeyScore for Multimodal Frame Scoring and Video-Language Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T23:02:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:12:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T07:42:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:30:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Selecting informative keyframes is critical for efficient video understanding, yet existing approaches often rely on heuristics, ignore semantics, or produce redundant frames. We propose KeyScore, a caption-aware frame scoring method that combines three complementary signals: semantic similarity to captions, temporal representativeness, and contextual drop impact. Applied to large-scale video-caption datasets, KeyScore generates frame-level importance scores that enable training keyframe extractors or guiding video-language models. To support this, we also propose STACFP, a Spatio-Temporal Adaptive Clustering method that generates diverse and compact frame proposals across long videos. Together, KeyScore and STACFP reduce uninformative frames while preserving critical content, resulting in faster and more accurate inference. Our experiments on three standard video-language benchmarks (MSRVTT, MSVD, DiDeMo) show that combining STACFP and KeyScore enables up to 99% frame reduction compared to full-frame processing, while outperforming uniform 8-frame encoders in video-text retrieval, keyframe extraction, and action recognition tasks. By focusing on semantically relevant frames, our method enhances both efficiency and performance, enabling scalable and caption-grounded video understanding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06509","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:47:56Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:47:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:18:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06870","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06870","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06870","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yining","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinman","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jinman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuangxin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Chuangxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhao","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Shuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gerald","familyName":"Penn","name":"Penn, Gerald","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shinan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"$λ$-GRPO: Unifying the GRPO Frameworks with Learnable Token Preferences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:39:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:40:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T03:27:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:25:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning with Human Feedback (RLHF) has been the dominant approach for improving the reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs). Recently, Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) has simplified this paradigm by replacing the reward and value models with rule-based verifiers. A prominent example is Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO). However, GRPO inherently suffers from a length bias, since the same advantage is uniformly assigned to all tokens of a response. As a result, longer responses distribute the reward over more tokens and thus contribute disproportionately to gradient updates. Several variants, such as DAPO and Dr. GRPO, modify the token-level aggregation of the loss, yet these methods remain heuristic and offer limited interpretability regarding their implicit token preferences. In this work, we explore the possibility of allowing the model to learn its own token preference during optimization. We unify existing frameworks under a single formulation and introduce a learnable parameter $λ$ that adaptively controls token-level weighting. We use $λ$-GRPO to denote our method, and we find that $λ$-GRPO achieves consistent improvements over vanilla GRPO and DAPO on multiple mathematical reasoning benchmarks. On Qwen2.5 models with 1.5B, 3B, and 7B parameters, $λ$-GRPO improves average accuracy by $+1.9\\%$, $+1.0\\%$, and $+1.7\\%$ compared to GRPO, respectively. Importantly, these gains come without any modifications to the training data or additional computational cost, highlighting the effectiveness and practicality of learning token preferences."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06870","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:56:23Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:56:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:40:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06825","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06825","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06825","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenpeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojie","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Xiaojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunye","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chunye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Tool Generation with Models as Tools and Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:48:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:37:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T04:38:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:28:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Tool-augmented language models have demonstrated strong capabilities, but their reliance on live API access creates scalability and reliability challenges during training and deployment. We propose MTR, a simulation-first training framework for tool-augmented reasoning. Instead of relying on live APIs, MTR learns from complete ReAct traces with schema-validated, simulated observations. Our approach operates through a multi-agent architecture where a ToolMaker generates task-specific, OpenAI-compatible tool interfaces, an AutoAgent produces structured think-act-observe sequences, and a ToolActor simulates realistic responses. Training proceeds in two stages: Stage-1 Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) teaches 'trace grammar' from complete reasoning sequences; Stage-2 Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) optimizes strategy with a composite trace reward that balances answer correctness and internal consistency. Across four multi-hop QA benchmarks (HotpotQA, MuSiQue, 2WikiMultiHopQA, Bamboogle), MTR attains competitive Exact Match (EM) scores to live-API systems and excels on reasoning-intensive tasks, suggesting that effective tool reasoning can be learned from structured traces without live interactions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06825","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:19Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:40:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06674","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06674","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06674","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cen Mia","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Cen Mia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiantian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tiantian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanchen","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Hanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufeng Wayne","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yufeng Wayne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaowei","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Shaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingzhi","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mingzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu Elaine","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu Elaine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeremy","familyName":"Werner","name":"Werner, Jeremy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claire Na","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Claire Na","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yashar","familyName":"Mehdad","name":"Mehdad, Yashar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agent-in-the-Loop: A Data Flywheel for Continuous Improvement in LLM-based Customer Support"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:57:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:26:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T02:01:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:20:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce an Agent-in-the-Loop (AITL) framework that implements a continuous data flywheel for iteratively improving an LLM-based customer support system. Unlike standard offline approaches that rely on batch annotations, AITL integrates four key types of annotations directly into live customer operations: (1) pairwise response preferences, (2) agent adoption and rationales, (3) knowledge relevance checks, and (4) identification of missing knowledge. These feedback signals seamlessly feed back into models' updates, reducing retraining cycles from months to weeks. Our production pilot involving US-based customer support agents demonstrated significant improvements in retrieval accuracy (+11.7% recall@75, +14.8% precision@8), generation quality (+8.4% helpfulness) and agent adoption rates (+4.5%). These results underscore the effectiveness of embedding human feedback loops directly into operational workflows to continuously refine LLM-based customer support system."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Industry Track submission (Paper #305). Preprint. Main text within the 7-page industry limit (references/appendices excluded). Contains multiple figures and tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06674","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:47Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:40:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06223","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06223","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06223","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hans G. W.","familyName":"van Dam","name":"van Dam, Hans G. W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Multimodal GUI Architecture for Interfacing with LLM-Based Conversational Assistants"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; D.2.11","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-31T14:40:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T12:55:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:53:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Advances in large language models (LLMs) and real-time speech recognition now make it possible to issue any graphical user interface (GUI) action through natural language and receive the corresponding system response directly through the GUI. Most production applications were never designed with speech in mind. This article provides a concrete architecture that enables GUIs to interface with LLM-based speech-enabled assistants.\n The architecture makes an application's navigation graph and semantics available through the Model Context Protocol (MCP). The ViewModel, part of the MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel) pattern, exposes the application's capabilities to the assistant by supplying both tools applicable to a currently visible view and application-global tools extracted from the GUI tree router. This architecture facilitates full voice accessibility while ensuring reliable alignment between spoken input and the visual interface, accompanied by consistent feedback across modalities. It future-proofs apps for upcoming OS super assistants that employ computer use agents (CUAs) and natively consume MCP if an application provides it.\n To address concerns about privacy and data security, the practical effectiveness of locally deployable, open-weight LLMs for speech-enabled multimodal UIs is evaluated. Findings suggest that recent smaller open-weight models approach the performance of leading proprietary models in overall accuracy and require enterprise-grade hardware for fast responsiveness.\n A demo implementation of the proposed architecture can be found at https://github.com/hansvdam/langbar"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 19 figures, code available at https://github.com/hansvdam/langbar"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06223","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:17Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:39:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07307","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07307","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07307","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rushi","familyName":"Qiang","name":"Qiang, Rushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anikait","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Anikait","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Percy","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Percy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sherry","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Sherry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MLE-Smith: Scaling MLE Tasks with Automated Multi-Agent Pipeline"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:57:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:04:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Language Models (LMs) have made significant progress in automating machine learning engineering (MLE), the acquisition of high-quality MLE training data is significantly constrained. Current MLE benchmarks suffer from low scalability and limited applicability because they rely on static, manually curated tasks, demanding extensive time and manual effort to produce. We introduce MLE-Smith, a fully automated multi-agent pipeline, to transform raw datasets into competition-style MLE challenges through an efficient generate-verify-execute paradigm for scaling MLE tasks with verifiable quality, real-world usability, and rich diversity. The proposed multi-agent pipeline in MLE-Smith drives structured task design and standardized refactoring, coupled with a hybrid verification mechanism that enforces strict structural rules and high-level semantic soundness. It further validates empirical solvability and real-world fidelity through interactive execution. We apply MLE-Smith to 224 of real-world datasets and generate 606 tasks spanning multiple categories, objectives, and modalities, demonstrating that MLE-Smith can work effectively across a wide range of real-world datasets. Evaluation on the generated tasks shows that the performance of eight mainstream and cutting-edge LLMs on MLE-Smith tasks is strongly correlated with their performance on carefully human-designed tasks, highlighting the effectiveness of the MLE-Smith to scaling up MLE tasks, while maintaining task quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07307","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:48Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:06:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07293","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07293","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07293","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peize","familyName":"He","name":"He, Peize","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichen","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yubo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoqian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaoqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jiajie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehui","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Zehui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuangcheng","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Zhuangcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangqi","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Xiangqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiabing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiabing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cunxiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Cunxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Conghui","familyName":"He","name":"He, Conghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AudioMarathon: A Comprehensive Benchmark for Long-Context Audio Understanding and Efficiency in Audio LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:50:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:03:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Processing long-form audio is a major challenge for Large Audio Language models (LALMs). These models struggle with the quadratic cost of attention ($O(N^2)$) and with modeling long-range temporal dependencies. Existing audio benchmarks are built mostly from short clips and do not evaluate models in realistic long context settings. To address this gap, we introduce AudioMarathon, a benchmark designed to evaluate both understanding and inference efficiency on long-form audio. AudioMarathon provides a diverse set of tasks built upon three pillars: long-context audio inputs with durations ranging from 90.0 to 300.0 seconds, which correspond to encoded sequences of 2,250 to 7,500 audio tokens, respectively, full domain coverage across speech, sound, and music, and complex reasoning that requires multi-hop inference. We evaluate state-of-the-art LALMs and observe clear performance drops as audio length grows. We also study acceleration techniques and analyze the trade-offs of token pruning and KV cache eviction. The results show large gaps across current LALMs and highlight the need for better temporal reasoning and memory-efficient architectures. We believe AudioMarathon will drive the audio and multimodal research community to develop more advanced audio understanding models capable of solving complex audio tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages, 23 figures, the code is available at \\url{https://github.com/DabDans/AudioMarathon}"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07293","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:28Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:06:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07243","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07243","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07243","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Enguehard","name":"Enguehard, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Morgane","familyName":"Van Ermengem","name":"Van Ermengem, Morgane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kate","familyName":"Atkinson","name":"Atkinson, Kate","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujeong","familyName":"Cha","name":"Cha, Sujeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arijit Ghosh","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Arijit Ghosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prashanth Kallur","familyName":"Ramaswamy","name":"Ramaswamy, Prashanth Kallur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeremy","familyName":"Roghair","name":"Roghair, Jeremy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hannah R","familyName":"Marlowe","name":"Marlowe, Hannah R","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carina Suzana","familyName":"Negreanu","name":"Negreanu, Carina Suzana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kitty","familyName":"Boxall","name":"Boxall, Kitty","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diana","familyName":"Mincu","name":"Mincu, Diana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LeMAJ (Legal LLM-as-a-Judge): Bridging Legal Reasoning and LLM Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:10:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:01:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluating large language model (LLM) outputs in the legal domain presents unique challenges due to the complex and nuanced nature of legal analysis. Current evaluation approaches either depend on reference data, which is costly to produce, or use standardized assessment methods, both of which have significant limitations for legal applications.\n Although LLM-as-a-Judge has emerged as a promising evaluation technique, its reliability and effectiveness in legal contexts depend heavily on evaluation processes unique to the legal industry and how trustworthy the evaluation appears to the human legal expert. This is where existing evaluation methods currently fail and exhibit considerable variability.\n This paper aims to close the gap: a) we break down lengthy responses into 'Legal Data Points' (LDPs), self-contained units of information, and introduce a novel, reference-free evaluation methodology that reflects how lawyers evaluate legal answers; b) we demonstrate that our method outperforms a variety of baselines on both our proprietary dataset and an open-source dataset (LegalBench); c) we show how our method correlates more closely with human expert evaluations and helps improve inter-annotator agreement; and finally d) we open source our Legal Data Points for a subset of LegalBench used in our experiments, allowing the research community to replicate our results and advance research in this vital area of LLM evaluation on legal question-answering."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in Natural Legal Language Processing - EMNLP Workshop 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07243","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:16Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:05:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07176","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07176","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07176","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhao","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Xinhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyi","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Zhongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianping","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jianping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exposing LLM User Privacy via Traffic Fingerprint Analysis: A Study of Privacy Risks in LLM Agent Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:16:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:57:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as agents that orchestrate tasks and integrate external tools to execute complex workflows. We demonstrate that these interactive behaviors leave distinctive fingerprints in encrypted traffic exchanged between users and LLM agents. By analyzing traffic patterns associated with agent workflows and tool invocations, adversaries can infer agent activities, distinguish specific agents, and even profile sensitive user attributes. To highlight this risk, we develop AgentPrint, which achieves an F1-score of 0.866 in agent identification and attains 73.9% and 69.1% top-3 accuracy in user attribute inference for simulated- and real-user settings, respectively. These results uncover an overlooked risk: the very interactivity that empowers LLM agents also exposes user privacy, underscoring the urgent need for technical countermeasures alongside regulatory and policy safeguards."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages with 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07176","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:40Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:03:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07173","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07173","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07173","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Tawkat Islam","familyName":"Khondaker","name":"Khondaker, Md Tawkat Islam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julia","familyName":"Harrington","name":"Harrington, Julia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shady","familyName":"Shehata","name":"Shehata, Shady","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NurseLLM: The First Specialized Language Model for Nursing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:15:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:57:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) have significantly transformed medical systems. However, their potential within specialized domains such as nursing remains largely underexplored. In this work, we introduce NurseLLM, the first nursing-specialized LLM tailored for multiple choice question-answering (MCQ) tasks. We develop a multi-stage data generation pipeline to build the first large scale nursing MCQ dataset to train LLMs on a broad spectrum of nursing topics. We further introduce multiple nursing benchmarks to enable rigorous evaluation. Our extensive experiments demonstrate that NurseLLM outperforms SoTA general-purpose and medical-specialized LLMs of comparable size on different benchmarks, underscoring the importance of a specialized LLM for the nursing domain. Finally, we explore the role of reasoning and multi-agent collaboration systems in nursing, highlighting their promise for future research and applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Industry Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07173","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:36Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:03:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07169","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07169","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07169","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yike","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simin","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Simin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ziqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shifan","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Shifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dahua","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Dahua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"More Data or Better Data? A Critical Analysis of Data Selection and Synthesis for Mathematical Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:07:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:57:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) play a critical role in many downstream tasks, yet depend strongly on the quality of training data. Despite various proposed data construction methods, their practical utility in real-world pipelines remains underexplored. In this work, we conduct a comprehensive analysis of open-source datasets and data synthesis techniques for mathematical reasoning, evaluating them under a unified pipeline designed to mirror training and deployment scenarios. We further distill effective data selection strategies and identify practical methods suitable for industrial applications. Our findings highlight that structuring data in more interpretable formats, or distilling from stronger models often outweighs simply scaling up data volume. This study provides actionable guidance for integrating training data to enhance LLM capabilities, supporting both cost-effective data curation and scalable model enhancement. We hope this work will inspire further research on how to balance \"more data\" versus \"better data\" for real-world reasoning tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 3 figures, submitted to EMNLP 2025 Industry Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07169","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:30Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:03:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07147","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07147","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07147","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arshika","familyName":"Lalan","name":"Lalan, Arshika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajat","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Rajat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Kolsur","name":"Kolsur, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debojyoti","familyName":"Dutta","name":"Dutta, Debojyoti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Multi-Agent Framework for Stateful Inference-Time Search"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:48:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:56:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent work explores agentic inference-time techniques to perform structured, multi-step reasoning. However, stateless inference often struggles on multi-step tasks due to the absence of persistent state. Moreover, task-specific fine-tuning or instruction-tuning often achieve surface-level code generation but remain brittle on tasks requiring deeper reasoning and long-horizon dependencies. To address these limitations, we propose stateful multi-agent evolutionary search, a training-free framework that departs from prior stateless approaches by combining (i) persistent inference-time state, (ii) adversarial mutation, and (iii) evolutionary preservation. We demonstrate its effectiveness in automated unit test generation through the generation of edge cases. We generate robust edge cases using an evolutionary search process, where specialized agents sequentially propose, mutate, and score candidates. A controller maintains persistent state across generations, while evolutionary preservation ensures diversity and exploration across all possible cases. This yields a generalist agent capable of discovering robust, high-coverage edge cases across unseen codebases. Experiments show our stateful multi-agent inference framework achieves substantial gains in coverage over stateless single-step baselines, evaluated on prevalent unit-testing benchmarks such as HumanEval and TestGenEvalMini and using three diverse LLM families - Llama, Gemma, and GPT. These results indicate that combining persistent inference-time state with evolutionary search materially improves unit-test generation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07147","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:02:59Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:03:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07098","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07098","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07098","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guo","familyName":"Yutong","name":"Yutong, Guo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanying","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wanying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichen","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Zichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TALENT: Table VQA via Augmented Language-Enhanced Natural-text Transcription"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:56:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:52:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Table Visual Question Answering (Table VQA) is typically addressed by large vision-language models (VLMs). While such models can answer directly from images, they often miss fine-grained details unless scaled to very large sizes, which are computationally prohibitive, especially for mobile deployment. A lighter alternative is to have a small VLM perform OCR and then use a large language model (LLM) to reason over structured outputs such as Markdown tables. However, these representations are not naturally optimized for LLMs and still introduce substantial errors. We propose TALENT (Table VQA via Augmented Language-Enhanced Natural-text Transcription), a lightweight framework that leverages dual representations of tables. TALENT prompts a small VLM to produce both OCR text and natural language narration, then combines them with the question for reasoning by an LLM. This reframes Table VQA as an LLM-centric multimodal reasoning task, where the VLM serves as a perception-narration module rather than a monolithic solver. Additionally, we construct ReTabVQA, a more challenging Table VQA dataset requiring multi-step quantitative reasoning over table images. Experiments show that TALENT enables a small VLM-LLM combination to match or surpass a single large VLM at significantly lower computational cost on both public datasets and ReTabVQA."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07098","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:01:49Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:01:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:01:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07092","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07092","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07092","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riccardo","familyName":"Mereu","name":"Mereu, Riccardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aidan","familyName":"Scannell","name":"Scannell, Aidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Jitta","name":"Jitta, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Dominguez","name":"Dominguez, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luigi","familyName":"Acerbi","name":"Acerbi, Luigi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amos","familyName":"Storkey","name":"Storkey, Amos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generative World Modelling for Humanoids: 1X World Model Challenge Technical Report"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:49:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:52:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"World models are a powerful paradigm in AI and robotics, enabling agents to reason about the future by predicting visual observations or compact latent states. The 1X World Model Challenge introduces an open-source benchmark of real-world humanoid interaction, with two complementary tracks: sampling, focused on forecasting future image frames, and compression, focused on predicting future discrete latent codes. For the sampling track, we adapt the video generation foundation model Wan-2.2 TI2V-5B to video-state-conditioned future frame prediction. We condition the video generation on robot states using AdaLN-Zero, and further post-train the model using LoRA. For the compression track, we train a Spatio-Temporal Transformer model from scratch. Our models achieve 23.0 dB PSNR in the sampling task and a Top-500 CE of 6.6386 in the compression task, securing 1st place in both challenges."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 3 figures, 1X world model challenge technical report"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07092","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:01:41Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:01:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:01:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07091","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07091","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07091","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baixuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Baixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaowei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong Ting","familyName":"Tsang","name":"Tsang, Hong Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianqing","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Tianqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangqiu","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yangqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Cognitive Bandwidth Bottleneck: Shifting Long-Horizon Agent from Planning with Actions to Planning with Schemas"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:47:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:52:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Enabling LLMs to effectively operate long-horizon task which requires long-term planning and multiple interactions is essential for open-world autonomy. Conventional methods adopt planning with actions where a executable action list would be provided as reference. However, this action representation choice would be impractical when the environment action space is combinatorial exploded (e.g., open-ended real world). This naturally leads to a question: As environmental action space scales, what is the optimal action representation for long-horizon agents? In this paper, we systematically study the effectiveness of two different action representations. The first one is conventional planning with actions (PwA) which is predominantly adopted for its effectiveness on existing benchmarks. The other one is planning with schemas (PwS) which instantiate an action schema into action lists (e.g., \"move [OBJ] to [OBJ]\" -\u0026gt; \"move apple to desk\") to ensure concise action space and reliable scalability. This alternative is motivated by its alignment with human cognition and its compliance with environment-imposed action format restriction. We propose cognitive bandwidth perspective as a conceptual framework to qualitatively understand the differences between these two action representations and empirically observe a representation-choice inflection point between ALFWorld (~35 actions) and SciWorld (~500 actions), which serve as evidence of the need for scalable representations. We further conduct controlled experiments to study how the location of this inflection point interacts with different model capacities: stronger planning proficiency shifts the inflection rightward, whereas better schema instantiation shifts it leftward. Finally, noting the suboptimal performance of PwS agents, we provide an actionable guide for building more capable PwS agents for better scalable autonomy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07091","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:01:39Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:01:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:01:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07064","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07064","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07064","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manh Hung","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Manh Hung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Tschiatschek","name":"Tschiatschek, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adish","familyName":"Singla","name":"Singla, Adish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompt Optimization Across Multiple Agents for Representing Diverse Human Populations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:28:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:51:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The difficulty and expense of obtaining large-scale human responses make Large Language Models (LLMs) an attractive alternative and a promising proxy for human behavior. However, prior work shows that LLMs often produce homogeneous outputs that fail to capture the rich diversity of human perspectives and behaviors. Thus, rather than trying to capture this diversity with a single LLM agent, we propose a novel framework to construct a set of agents that collectively capture the diversity of a given human population. Each agent is an LLM whose behavior is steered by conditioning on a small set of human demonstrations (task-response pairs) through in-context learning. The central challenge is therefore to select a representative set of LLM agents from the exponentially large space of possible agents. We tackle this selection problem from the lens of submodular optimization. In particular, we develop methods that offer different trade-offs regarding time complexity and performance guarantees. Extensive experiments in crowdsourcing and educational domains demonstrate that our approach constructs agents that more effectively represent human populations compared to baselines. Moreover, behavioral analyses on new tasks show that these agents reproduce the behavior patterns and perspectives of the students and annotators they are designed to represent."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07064","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:01:01Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:01:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:01:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06999","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06999","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06999","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus","familyName":"Reuter","name":"Reuter, Markus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobias","familyName":"Lingenberg","name":"Lingenberg, Tobias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rūta","familyName":"Liepiņa","name":"Liepiņa, Rūta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesca","familyName":"Lagioia","name":"Lagioia, Francesca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Lippi","name":"Lippi, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni","familyName":"Sartor","name":"Sartor, Giovanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Passerini","name":"Passerini, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Burcu","familyName":"Sayin","name":"Sayin, Burcu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Reliable Retrieval in RAG Systems for Large Legal Datasets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; H.3.3; K.5.0","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:22:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:47:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is a promising approach to mitigate hallucinations in Large Language Models (LLMs) for legal applications, but its reliability is critically dependent on the accuracy of the retrieval step. This is particularly challenging in the legal domain, where large databases of structurally similar documents often cause retrieval systems to fail. In this paper, we address this challenge by first identifying and quantifying a critical failure mode we term Document-Level Retrieval Mismatch (DRM), where the retriever selects information from entirely incorrect source documents. To mitigate DRM, we investigate a simple and computationally efficient technique which we refer to as Summary-Augmented Chunking (SAC). This method enhances each text chunk with a document-level synthetic summary, thereby injecting crucial global context that would otherwise be lost during a standard chunking process. Our experiments on a diverse set of legal information retrieval tasks show that SAC greatly reduces DRM and, consequently, also improves text-level retrieval precision and recall. Interestingly, we find that a generic summarization strategy outperforms an approach that incorporates legal expert domain knowledge to target specific legal elements. Our work provides evidence that this practical, scalable, and easily integrable technique enhances the reliability of RAG systems when applied to large-scale legal document datasets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for the 7th Natural Legal Language Processing Workshop (NLLP 2025), co-located with EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06999","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:59:26Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:59:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:59:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06994","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06994","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06994","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Artur","familyName":"Horal","name":"Horal, Artur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Pina","name":"Pina, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henrique","familyName":"Paz","name":"Paz, Henrique","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iago","familyName":"Paulo","name":"Paulo, Iago","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"João","familyName":"Soares","name":"Soares, João","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafael","familyName":"Ferreira","name":"Ferreira, Rafael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diogo","familyName":"Tavares","name":"Tavares, Diogo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diogo","familyName":"Glória-Silva","name":"Glória-Silva, Diogo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"João","familyName":"Magalhães","name":"Magalhães, João","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Semedo","name":"Semedo, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:18:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:46:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents the vision, scientific contributions, and technical details of RedTWIZ: an adaptive and diverse multi-turn red teaming framework, to audit the robustness of Large Language Models (LLMs) in AI-assisted software development. Our work is driven by three major research streams: (1) robust and systematic assessment of LLM conversational jailbreaks; (2) a diverse generative multi-turn attack suite, supporting compositional, realistic and goal-oriented jailbreak conversational strategies; and (3) a hierarchical attack planner, which adaptively plans, serializes, and triggers attacks tailored to specific LLM's vulnerabilities. Together, these contributions form a unified framework -- combining assessment, attack generation, and strategic planning -- to comprehensively evaluate and expose weaknesses in LLMs' robustness. Extensive evaluation is conducted to systematically assess and analyze the performance of the overall system and each component. Experimental results demonstrate that our multi-turn adversarial attack strategies can successfully lead state-of-the-art LLMs to produce unsafe generations, highlighting the pressing need for more research into enhancing LLM's robustness."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06994","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:59:19Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:59:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:59:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06954","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06954","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06954","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng-An","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zheng-An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Condensation to Rank Collapse: A Two-Stage Analysis of Transformer Training Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T12:37:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:45:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although transformer-based models have shown exceptional empirical performance, the fundamental principles governing their training dynamics are inadequately characterized beyond configuration-specific studies. Inspired by empirical evidence showing improved reasoning capabilities under small initialization scales in language models, we employ the gradient flow analytical framework established in [Zhou et al. NeurIPS 2022] to systematically investigate linearized Transformer training dynamics. Our theoretical analysis dissects the dynamics of attention modules into two distinct stages. In the first stage, asymmetric weight perturbations from random initialization sustain non-degenerate gradient dynamics in parameter matrices, facilitating systematic escape from small initialization regimes. Subsequently, these matrices undergo condensation, progressively aligning toward the target orientation. In the second stage, the previously static key-query matrices actively participate in training, driving the normalized matrices toward asymptotic rank collapse. This two-stage framework generalizes classical directional convergence results."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06954","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:58:21Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:58:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:58:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06912","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06912","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06912","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandros","familyName":"Vassiliades","name":"Vassiliades, Alexandros","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolaos","familyName":"Polatidis","name":"Polatidis, Nikolaos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stamatios","familyName":"Samaras","name":"Samaras, Stamatios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sotiris","familyName":"Diplaris","name":"Diplaris, Sotiris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ignacio Cabrera","familyName":"Martin","name":"Martin, Ignacio Cabrera","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yannis","familyName":"Manolopoulos","name":"Manolopoulos, Yannis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefanos","familyName":"Vrochidis","name":"Vrochidis, Stefanos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ioannis","familyName":"Kompatsiaris","name":"Kompatsiaris, Ioannis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Utilizing Large Language Models for Machine Learning Explainability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T11:46:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:42:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study explores the explainability capabilities of large language models (LLMs), when employed to autonomously generate machine learning (ML) solutions. We examine two classification tasks: (i) a binary classification problem focused on predicting driver alertness states, and (ii) a multilabel classification problem based on the yeast dataset. Three state-of-the-art LLMs (i.e. OpenAI GPT, Anthropic Claude, and DeepSeek) are prompted to design training pipelines for four common classifiers: Random Forest, XGBoost, Multilayer Perceptron, and Long Short-Term Memory networks. The generated models are evaluated in terms of predictive performance (recall, precision, and F1-score) and explainability using SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations). Specifically, we measure Average SHAP Fidelity (Mean Squared Error between SHAP approximations and model outputs) and Average SHAP Sparsity (number of features deemed influential). The results reveal that LLMs are capable of producing effective and interpretable models, achieving high fidelity and consistent sparsity, highlighting their potential as automated tools for interpretable ML pipeline generation. The results show that LLMs can produce effective, interpretable pipelines with high fidelity and consistent sparsity, closely matching manually engineered baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06912","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:57:22Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:57:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:57:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06911","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06911","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06911","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hacane","familyName":"Hechehouche","name":"Hechehouche, Hacane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andre","familyName":"Antakli","name":"Antakli, Andre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Klusch","name":"Klusch, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Assisted Modeling of Semantic Web-Enabled Multi-Agents Systems with AJAN"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T11:45:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:42:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"There are many established semantic Web standards for implementing multi-agent driven applications. The AJAN framework allows to engineer multi-agent systems based on these standards. In particular, agent knowledge is represented in RDF/RDFS and OWL, while agent behavior models are defined with Behavior Trees and SPARQL to access and manipulate this knowledge. However, the appropriate definition of RDF/RDFS and SPARQL-based agent behaviors still remains a major hurdle not only for agent modelers in practice. For example, dealing with URIs is very error-prone regarding typos and dealing with complex SPARQL queries in large-scale environments requires a high learning curve. In this paper, we present an integrated development environment to overcome such hurdles of modeling AJAN agents and at the same time to extend the user community for AJAN by the possibility to leverage Large Language Models for agent engineering."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06911","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:57:20Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:57:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:57:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06903","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06903","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06903","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenwen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangnan","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Guangnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Machines Meet Each Other: Network Effects and the Strategic Role of History in Multi-Agent AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Economics (econ.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T11:39:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:42:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As artificial intelligence (AI) enters the agentic era, large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as autonomous agents that interact with one another rather than operate in isolation. This shift raises a fundamental question: how do machine agents behave in interdependent environments where outcomes depend not only on their own choices but also on the coordinated expectations of peers? To address this question, we study LLM agents in a canonical network-effect game, where economic theory predicts convergence to a fulfilled expectation equilibrium (FEE). We design an experimental framework in which 50 heterogeneous GPT-5-based agents repeatedly interact under systematically varied network-effect strengths, price trajectories, and decision-history lengths. The results reveal that LLM agents systematically diverge from FEE: they underestimate participation at low prices, overestimate at high prices, and sustain persistent dispersion. Crucially, the way history is structured emerges as a design lever. Simple monotonic histories-where past outcomes follow a steady upward or downward trend-help stabilize coordination, whereas nonmonotonic histories amplify divergence and path dependence. Regression analyses at the individual level further show that price is the dominant driver of deviation, history moderates this effect, and network effects amplify contextual distortions. Together, these findings advance machine behavior research by providing the first systematic evidence on multi-agent AI systems under network effects and offer guidance for configuring such systems in practice."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06903","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:57:09Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:57:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:57:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06878","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06878","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06878","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daria","familyName":"Ozerova","name":"Ozerova, Daria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ekaterina","familyName":"Trofimova","name":"Trofimova, Ekaterina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TGPR: Tree-Guided Policy Refinement for Robust Self-Debugging of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:47:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:40:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Iterative refinement has been a promising paradigm to enable large language models (LLMs) to resolve difficult reasoning and problem-solving tasks. One of the key challenges, however, is how to effectively search through the enormous search space of possible refinements. Existing methods typically fall back on predefined heuristics, which are troubled by the exploration-exploitation dilemma and cannot adapt based on past refinement outcomes. We introduce Tree-Guided Policy Refinement (TGPR), a novel framework that combines GRPO with a Thompson-Sampling-based tree search. TGPR explores both failed and successful refinement paths actively, with denser training trajectories and more adaptive policies. On HumanEval, MBPP, and APPS benchmarks, our method achieves up to +4.2 percentage points absolute improvement in pass@1 (on MBPP) and up to +12.51 percentage points absolute improvement in pass@10 (on APPS) compared to a competitive GRPO baseline. Apart from debugging code, TGPR focuses on a principled approach to combining learned policies with structured search methods, offering a general framework for enhancing iterative refinement and stateful reasoning in LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06878","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:56:34Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:56:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:56:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06872","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06872","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06872","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frederic","familyName":"Gmeiner","name":"Gmeiner, Frederic","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenneth","familyName":"Holstein","name":"Holstein, Kenneth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolas","familyName":"Martelaro","name":"Martelaro, Nikolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prototyping Multimodal GenAI Real-Time Agents with Counterfactual Replays and Hybrid Wizard-of-Oz"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:39:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:40:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in multimodal generative AI (GenAI) enable the creation of personal context-aware real-time agents that, for example, can augment user workflows by following their on-screen activities and providing contextual assistance. However, prototyping such experiences is challenging, especially when supporting people with domain-specific tasks using real-time inputs such as speech and screen recordings. While prototyping an LLM-based proactive support agent system, we found that existing prototyping and evaluation methods were insufficient to anticipate the nuanced situational complexity and contextual immediacy required. To overcome these challenges, we explored a novel user-centered prototyping approach that combines counterfactual video replay prompting and hybrid Wizard-of-Oz methods to iteratively design and refine agent behaviors. This paper discusses our prototyping experiences, highlighting successes and limitations, and offers a practical guide and an open-source toolkit for UX designers, HCI researchers, and AI toolmakers to build more user-centered and context-aware multimodal agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 5 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06872","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:56:26Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:56:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:56:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06857","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06857","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06857","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jianing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianfei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deyang","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Deyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangzhou","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xiangzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Xi","name":"Xi, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xunliang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Xunliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shikun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Autoformalizer with Tool Feedback"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:25:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:39:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autoformalization addresses the scarcity of data for Automated Theorem Proving (ATP) by translating mathematical problems from natural language into formal statements. Efforts in recent work shift from directly prompting large language models to training an end-to-end formalizer model from scratch, achieving remarkable advancements. However, existing formalizer still struggles to consistently generate valid statements that meet syntactic validity and semantic consistency. To address this issue, we propose the Autoformalizer with Tool Feedback (ATF), a novel approach that incorporates syntactic and consistency information as tools into the formalization process. By integrating Lean 4 compilers for syntax corrections and employing a multi-LLMs-as-judge approach for consistency validation, the model is able to adaptively refine generated statements according to the tool feedback, enhancing both syntactic validity and semantic consistency. The training of ATF involves a cold-start phase on synthetic tool-calling data, an expert iteration phase to improve formalization capabilities, and Direct Preference Optimization to alleviate ineffective revisions. Experimental results show that ATF markedly outperforms a range of baseline formalizer models, with its superior performance further validated by human evaluations. Subsequent analysis reveals that ATF demonstrates excellent inference scaling properties. Moreover, we open-source Numina-ATF, a dataset containing 750K synthetic formal statements to facilitate advancements in autoformalization and ATP research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06857","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:56:05Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:56:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:56:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06847","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06847","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06847","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pontakorn","familyName":"Trakuekul","name":"Trakuekul, Pontakorn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Attapol T.","familyName":"Rutherford","name":"Rutherford, Attapol T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jullajak","familyName":"Karnjanaekarin","name":"Karnjanaekarin, Jullajak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Narongkorn","familyName":"Panitsrisit","name":"Panitsrisit, Narongkorn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumana","familyName":"Sumanakul","name":"Sumanakul, Sumana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OpenJAI-v1.0: An Open Thai Large Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:12:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:38:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce OpenJAI-v1.0, an open-source large language model for Thai and English, developed from the Qwen3-14B model. Our work focuses on boosting performance on practical tasks through carefully curated data across three key use cases: instruction following, long-context understanding, and tool use. Evaluation results show that OpenJAI-v1.0 improves on the capabilities of its base model and outperforms other leading open-source Thai models on a diverse suite of benchmarks, while avoiding catastrophic forgetting. OpenJAI-v1.0 is publicly released as another alternative NLP resource for the Thai AI community."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06847","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:50Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:55:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06838","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06838","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06838","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elena","familyName":"Senger","name":"Senger, Elena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuri","familyName":"Campbell","name":"Campbell, Yuri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rob","familyName":"van der Goot","name":"van der Goot, Rob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Barbara","familyName":"Plank","name":"Plank, Barbara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Crossing Domains without Labels: Distant Supervision for Term Extraction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:02:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:38:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automatic Term Extraction (ATE) is a critical component in downstream NLP tasks such as document tagging, ontology construction and patent analysis. Current state-of-the-art methods require expensive human annotation and struggle with domain transfer, limiting their practical deployment. This highlights the need for more robust, scalable solutions and realistic evaluation settings. To address this, we introduce a comprehensive benchmark spanning seven diverse domains, enabling performance evaluation at both the document- and corpus-levels. Furthermore, we propose a robust LLM-based model that outperforms both supervised cross-domain encoder models and few-shot learning baselines and performs competitively with its GPT-4o teacher on this benchmark. The first step of our approach is generating psuedo-labels with this black-box LLM on general and scientific domains to ensure generalizability. Building on this data, we fine-tune the first LLMs for ATE. To further enhance document-level consistency, oftentimes needed for downstream tasks, we introduce lightweight post-hoc heuristics. Our approach exceeds previous approaches on 5/7 domains with an average improvement of 10 percentage points. We release our dataset and fine-tuned models to support future research in this area."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP Industry Track 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06838","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:37Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:55:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06828","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06828","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06828","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Keiblinger","name":"Keiblinger, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Recurrence-Complete Frame-based Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:50:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:37:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, attention-like mechanisms have been used to great success in the space of large language models, unlocking scaling potential to a previously unthinkable extent. \"Attention Is All You Need\" famously claims RNN cells are not needed in conjunction with attention. We challenge this view. In this paper, we point to existing proofs that architectures with fully parallelizable forward or backward passes cannot represent classes of problems specifically interesting for long-running agentic tasks. We further conjecture a critical time t beyond which non-recurrence-complete models fail to aggregate inputs correctly, with concrete implications for agentic systems (e.g., software engineering agents). To address this, we introduce a recurrence-complete architecture and train it on GitHub-derived action sequences. Loss follows a power law in the trained sequence length while the parameter count remains fixed. Moreover, longer-sequence training always amortizes its linearly increasing wall-time cost, yielding lower loss as a function of wall time."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06828","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:23Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:55:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06805","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06805","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06805","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"André","familyName":"Greiner-Petter","name":"Greiner-Petter, André","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maik","familyName":"Fröbe","name":"Fröbe, Maik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan Philip","familyName":"Wahle","name":"Wahle, Jan Philip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Terry","familyName":"Ruas","name":"Ruas, Terry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bela","familyName":"Gipp","name":"Gipp, Bela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akiko","familyName":"Aizawa","name":"Aizawa, Akiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Potthast","name":"Potthast, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Overview of the Plagiarism Detection Task at PAN 2025"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:33:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:36:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The generative plagiarism detection task at PAN 2025 aims at identifying automatically generated textual plagiarism in scientific articles and aligning them with their respective sources. We created a novel large-scale dataset of automatically generated plagiarism using three large language models: Llama, DeepSeek-R1, and Mistral. In this task overview paper, we outline the creation of this dataset, summarize and compare the results of all participants and four baselines, and evaluate the results on the last plagiarism detection task from PAN 2015 in order to interpret the robustness of the proposed approaches. We found that the current iteration does not invite a large variety of approaches as naive semantic similarity approaches based on embedding vectors provide promising results of up to 0.8 recall and 0.5 precision. In contrast, most of these approaches underperform significantly on the 2015 dataset, indicating a lack in generalizability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Working Notes at PAN at CLEF 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06805","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:51Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:54:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06774","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06774","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06774","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pierre","familyName":"Beckmann","name":"Beckmann, Pierre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Valentino","name":"Valentino, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"André","familyName":"Freitas","name":"Freitas, André","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive LLM-Symbolic Reasoning via Dynamic Logical Solver Composition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T08:57:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:34:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neuro-symbolic NLP methods aim to leverage the complementary strengths of large language models and formal logical solvers. However, current approaches are mostly static in nature, i.e., the integration of a target solver is predetermined at design time, hindering the ability to employ diverse formal inference strategies. To address this, we introduce an adaptive, multi-paradigm, neuro-symbolic inference framework that: (1) automatically identifies formal reasoning strategies from problems expressed in natural language; and (2) dynamically selects and applies specialized formal logical solvers via autoformalization interfaces. Extensive experiments on individual and multi-paradigm reasoning tasks support the following conclusions: LLMs are effective at predicting the necessary formal reasoning strategies with an accuracy above 90 percent. This enables flexible integration with formal logical solvers, resulting in our framework outperforming competing baselines by 27 percent and 6 percent compared to GPT-4o and DeepSeek-V3.1, respectively. Moreover, adaptive reasoning can even positively impact pure LLM methods, yielding gains of 10, 5, and 6 percent on zero-shot, CoT, and symbolic CoT settings with GPT-4o. Finally, although smaller models struggle with adaptive neuro-symbolic reasoning, post-training offers a viable path to improvement. Overall, this work establishes the foundations for adaptive LLM-symbolic reasoning, offering a path forward for unifying material and formal inferences on heterogeneous reasoning challenges."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06774","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:07Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:54:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06750","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06750","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06750","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaeseong","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jaeseong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dayoung","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Dayoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"seung-won","familyName":"hwang","name":"hwang, seung-won","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T08:17:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:32:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Reasoning Models (LRMs) excel in structured tasks by emulating deliberate human reasoning but often suffer from overthinking, degrading performance and wasting resources. One possible baseline is to deploy both LLM and LRM, then route input by predicting whether it requires reasoning and may cause overthinking. However, deploying multiple models can be costly or impractical. We propose a superposed deployment strategy with a lightweight, training-free regulation to optimize inference by switching one model on and off. Instead of routing, we selectively unlearn from LRM at inference, scaling down computation while preserving reasoning. By analyzing the cumulative energy of singular values, we identify optimal low-rank projections to adjust reasoning just right."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06750","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:34Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:53:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06727","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06727","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06727","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Miao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiwei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Weiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihua","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Weihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhan","familyName":"Ling","name":"Ling, Zhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuesong","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Xuesong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiecao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiecao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scaling LLM Multi-turn RL with End-to-end Summarization-based Context Management"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:29:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:30:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study reinforcement learning (RL) fine-tuning of large language model (LLM) agents for long-horizon multi-turn tool use, where context length quickly becomes a fundamental bottleneck. Existing RL pipelines can suffer from degraded instruction following, excessive rollout costs, and most importantly, strict context limits. To address these challenges, we introduce summarization-based context management to training. In specific, it periodically compresses the tool using history by LLM-generated summaries that retain task-relevant information to keep a compact context while enabling the agent to scale beyond the fixed context window. Building on this formulation, we derive a policy gradient representation that seamlessly enables standard LLM RL infrastructures to optimize both tool-use behaviors as well as summarization strategies in an end-to-end fashion. We instantiate this framework with \\underline{SU}mmarization augmented \\underline{P}olicy \\underline{O}ptimization (\\texttt{SUPO}), an LLM RL algorithm that enables long-horizon training beyond a fixed context limit. Experiments on interactive function calling and searching tasks demonstrate that \\texttt{SUPO} significantly improves the success rate while maintaining the same or even lower working context length compared to baselines. We also demonstrate that for complex searching tasks, \\texttt{SUPO} can further improve the evaluation performance when scaling test-time maximum round of summarization beyond that of training time. Our results establish summarization-based context management as a principled and scalable approach for training RL agents beyond a fixed context length limit."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06727","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:02Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:53:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06664","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06664","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06664","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunzhong","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yunzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangmin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yangmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hewei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunlong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yunlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zora Zhiruo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zora Zhiruo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ToolMem: Enhancing Multimodal Agents with Learnable Tool Capability Memory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:32:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:25:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agents utilizing tools powered by large language models (LLMs) or vision-language models (VLMs) have demonstrated remarkable progress in diverse tasks across text and visual modalities. Unlike traditional tools such as calculators, which give deterministic outputs, neural tools perform uncertainly across task scenarios. While different tools for a task may excel in varied scenarios, existing agents typically rely on fixed tools, thus limiting the flexibility in selecting the most suitable tool for specific tasks. In contrast, humans snowball their understanding of the capabilities of different tools by interacting with them, and apply this knowledge to select the optimal tool when solving a future task. To build agents that similarly benefit from this process, we propose ToolMem that enables agents to develop memories of tool capabilities from previous interactions, by summarizing their strengths and weaknesses and storing them in memory; at inference, the agent can retrieve relevant entries from ToolMem, and select the best tool to solve individual tasks more accurately. We evaluate ToolMem on learning varied text generation and text-to-image generation neural tools. Compared to no-memory, generic agents, we find ToolMem-augmented agents predict tool performance 14.8% and 28.7% more accurately across text and multimodal generation scenarios. Moreover, ToolMem facilitates optimal tool selection among multiple choices by 21% and 24% absolute increases in respective scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06664","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:33Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:51:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06657","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06657","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06657","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyuan","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Boyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yueqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiloni","familyName":"Mehta","name":"Mehta, Hiloni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mick","familyName":"Zomnir","name":"Zomnir, Mick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omkar","familyName":"Pathak","name":"Pathak, Omkar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changping","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Changping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruolin","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Ruolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yajun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Yajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dapeng","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Dapeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xia","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Mingyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Onkar","familyName":"Dalal","name":"Dalal, Onkar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ningren","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Ningren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Powered Nuanced Video Attribute Annotation for Enhanced Recommendations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:17:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:25:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3705328.3748103","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a case study on deploying Large Language Models (LLMs) as an advanced \"annotation\" mechanism to achieve nuanced content understanding (e.g., discerning content \"vibe\") at scale within a large-scale industrial short-form video recommendation system. Traditional machine learning classifiers for content understanding face protracted development cycles and a lack of deep, nuanced comprehension. The \"LLM-as-annotators\" approach addresses these by significantly shortening development times and enabling the annotation of subtle attributes. This work details an end-to-end workflow encompassing: (1) iterative definition and robust evaluation of target attributes, refined by offline metrics and online A/B testing; (2) scalable offline bulk annotation of video corpora using LLMs with multimodal features, optimized inference, and knowledge distillation for broad application; and (3) integration of these rich annotations into the online recommendation serving system, for example, through personalized restrict retrieval. Experimental results demonstrate the efficacy of this approach, with LLMs outperforming human raters in offline annotation quality for nuanced attributes and yielding significant improvements of user participation and satisfied consumption in online A/B tests. The study provides insights into designing and scaling production-level LLM pipelines for rich content evaluation, highlighting the adaptability and benefits of LLM-generated nuanced understanding for enhancing content discovery, user satisfaction, and the overall effectiveness of modern recommendation systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"RecSys 2025 Industry Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06657","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:24Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:51:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06652","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06652","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06652","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangjian","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Shangjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhepei","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Zhepei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Lin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wei-Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Aligning Large Language Models via Fully Self-Synthetic Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:07:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:25:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Traditional reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) for large language models (LLMs) relies on expensive human-annotated datasets, while Reinforcement Learning from AI Feedback (RLAIF) also incurs significant costs, requiring the collection of diverse prompts and corresponding responses, often necessitating external reward models or proprietary models like GPT-4 to annotate preference pairs. In this work, we introduce Self-Alignment Optimization (SAO), a fully self-synthetic framework for LLM alignment, where all training data, including prompts (i.e., user queries), responses, and preferences, are generated by the model itself. Specifically, SAO first instructs the LLM to engage in persona role-play and generate diverse prompts and responses, which are then self-evaluated for preference optimization. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SAO effectively enhances the model's chat capabilities on standard benchmarks like AlpacaEval~2.0, while maintaining strong performance on downstream objective tasks (e.g., question-answering, math reasoning). Our work provides a practical solution for self-improvement in aligning LLMs, and the code for reproducing our results is available at: https://github.com/SJY8460/SAO."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06652","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:17Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:51:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06606","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06606","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06606","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Uswat","familyName":"Yusuf","name":"Yusuf, Uswat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Genevieve","familyName":"Caumartin","name":"Caumartin, Genevieve","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diego Elias","familyName":"Costa","name":"Costa, Diego Elias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond More Context: How Granularity and Order Drive Code Completion Quality"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T03:28:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:20:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Context plays an important role in the quality of code completion, as Large Language Models (LLMs) require sufficient and relevant information to assist developers in code generation tasks. However, composing a relevant context for code completion poses challenges in large repositories: First, the limited context length of LLMs makes it impractical to include all repository files. Second, the quality of generated code is highly sensitive to noisy or irrelevant context. In this paper, we present our approach for the ASE 2025 Context Collection Challenge. The challenge entails outperforming JetBrains baselines by designing effective retrieval and context collection strategies. We develop and evaluate a series of experiments that involve retrieval strategies at both the file and chunk levels. We focus our initial experiments on examining the impact of context size and file ordering on LLM performance. Our results show that the amount and order of context can significantly influence the performance of the models. We introduce chunk-based retrieval using static analysis, achieving a 6% improvement over our best file-retrieval strategy and a 16% improvement over the no-context baseline for Python in the initial phase of the competition. Our results highlight the importance of retrieval granularity, ordering and hybrid strategies in developing effective context collection pipelines for real-world development scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06606","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:50:10Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:50:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:50:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06587","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06587","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06587","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingbo","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jingbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bairu","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Bairu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujia","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Yujia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WebDART: Dynamic Decomposition and Re-planning for Complex Web Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T02:34:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:19:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) agents are becoming competent at straightforward web tasks, such as opening an item page or submitting a form, but still struggle with objectives that require long horizon navigation, large scale information extraction, and reasoning under constraints. We present WebDART, a general framework that enables a single LLM to handle such complex chores. WebDART (i) dynamically decomposes each objective into three focused subtasks: navigation, information extraction, and execution, so the model concentrates on one skill at a time, and (ii) continuously replans the decomposition as new webpages are revealed, taking advantage of newly discovered filters or shortcuts and avoiding redundant exploration. Evaluated on WebChoreArena, WebDART lifts success rates by up to 13.7 percentage points over previous SOTA agents, while matching their performance on the easier WebArena suite and completing tasks with up to 14.7 fewer navigation steps."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06587","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:49:44Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:49:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:49:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06579","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06579","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06579","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haofei","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Haofei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyang","familyName":"Xuan","name":"Xuan, Keyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fenghai","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Fenghai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunlun","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Kunlun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijie","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Zijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiaxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziheng","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Ziheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyle","familyName":"Richardson","name":"Richardson, Kyle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxuan","familyName":"You","name":"You, Jiaxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TinyScientist: An Interactive, Extensible, and Controllable Framework for Building Research Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T02:18:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:18:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automatic research with Large Language Models (LLMs) is rapidly gaining importance, driving the development of increasingly complex workflows involving multi-agent systems, planning, tool usage, code execution, and human-agent interaction to accelerate research processes. However, as more researchers and developers begin to use and build upon these tools and platforms, the complexity and difficulty of extending and maintaining such agentic workflows have become a significant challenge, particularly as algorithms and architectures continue to advance. To address this growing complexity, TinyScientist identifies the essential components of the automatic research workflow and proposes an interactive, extensible, and controllable framework that easily adapts to new tools and supports iterative growth. We provide an open-source codebase, an interactive web demonstration, and a PyPI Python package to make state-of-the-art auto-research pipelines broadly accessible to every researcher and developer."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, EMNLP 2025 Demo track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06579","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:49:33Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:49:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:49:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06565","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06565","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06565","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiuan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jiuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoxia","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Zhaoxia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Auto-Stega: An Agent-Driven System for Lifelong Strategy Evolution in LLM-Based Text Steganography"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T01:32:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:16:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid progress of LLMs, high quality generative text has become widely available as a cover for text steganography. However, prevailing methods rely on hand-crafted or pre-specified strategies and struggle to balance efficiency, imperceptibility, and security, particularly at high embedding rates. Accordingly, we propose Auto-Stega, an agent-driven self-evolving framework that is the first to realize self-evolving steganographic strategies by automatically discovering, composing, and adapting strategies at inference time; the framework operates as a closed loop of generating, evaluating, summarizing, and updating that continually curates a structured strategy library and adapts across corpora, styles, and task constraints. A decoding LLM recovers the information under the shared strategy. To handle high embedding rates, we introduce PC-DNTE, a plug-and-play algorithm that maintains alignment with the base model's conditional distribution at high embedding rates, preserving imperceptibility while enhancing security. Experimental results demonstrate that at higher embedding rates Auto-Stega achieves superior performance with gains of 42.2\\% in perplexity and 1.6\\% in anti-steganalysis performance over SOTA methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06565","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:49:13Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:49:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:49:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06559","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06559","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06559","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheonkam","familyName":"Jeong","name":"Jeong, Cheonkam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungdo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sungdo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jewoo","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Jewoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Algebra of Meaning: Why Machines Need Montague More Than Moore's Law"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T01:22:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:16:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Contemporary language models are fluent yet routinely mis-handle the types of meaning their outputs entail. We argue that hallucination, brittle moderation, and opaque compliance outcomes are symptoms of missing type-theoretic semantics rather than data or scale limitations. Building on Montague's view of language as typed, compositional algebra, we recast alignment as a parsing problem: natural-language inputs must be compiled into structures that make explicit their descriptive, normative, and legal dimensions under context.\n We present Savassan, a neuro-symbolic architecture that compiles utterances into Montague-style logical forms and maps them to typed ontologies extended with deontic operators and jurisdictional contexts. Neural components extract candidate structures from unstructured inputs; symbolic components perform type checking, constraint reasoning, and cross-jurisdiction mapping to produce compliance-aware guidance rather than binary censorship. In cross-border scenarios, the system \"parses once\" (e.g., defect claim(product x, company y)) and projects the result into multiple legal ontologies (e.g., defamation risk in KR/JP, protected opinion in US, GDPR checks in EU), composing outcomes into a single, explainable decision.\n This paper contributes: (i) a diagnosis of hallucination as a type error; (ii) a formal Montague-ontology bridge for business/legal reasoning; and (iii) a production-oriented design that embeds typed interfaces across the pipeline. We outline an evaluation plan using legal reasoning benchmarks and synthetic multi-jurisdiction suites. Our position is that trustworthy autonomy requires compositional typing of meaning, enabling systems to reason about what is described, what is prescribed, and what incurs liability within a unified algebra of meaning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06559","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:49:05Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:49:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:49:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06512","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06512","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06512","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avishree","familyName":"Khare","name":"Khare, Avishree","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hideki","familyName":"Okamoto","name":"Okamoto, Hideki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bardh","familyName":"Hoxha","name":"Hoxha, Bardh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgios","familyName":"Fainekos","name":"Fainekos, Georgios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajeev","familyName":"Alur","name":"Alur, Rajeev","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LogSTOP: Temporal Scores over Prediction Sequences for Matching and Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T23:05:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:12:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural models such as YOLO and HuBERT can be used to detect local properties such as objects (\"car\") and emotions (\"angry\") in individual frames of videos and audio clips respectively. The likelihood of these detections is indicated by scores in [0, 1]. Lifting these scores to temporal properties over sequences can be useful for several downstream applications such as query matching (e.g., \"does the speaker eventually sound happy in this audio clip?\"), and ranked retrieval (e.g., \"retrieve top 5 videos with a 10 second scene where a car is detected until a pedestrian is detected\"). In this work, we formalize this problem of assigning Scores for TempOral Properties (STOPs) over sequences, given potentially noisy score predictors for local properties. We then propose a scoring function called LogSTOP that can efficiently compute these scores for temporal properties represented in Linear Temporal Logic. Empirically, LogSTOP, with YOLO and HuBERT, outperforms Large Vision / Audio Language Models and other Temporal Logic-based baselines by at least 16% on query matching with temporal properties over objects-in-videos and emotions-in-speech respectively. Similarly, on ranked retrieval with temporal properties over objects and actions in videos, LogSTOP with Grounding DINO and SlowR50 reports at least a 19% and 16% increase in mean average precision and recall over zero-shot text-to-video retrieval baselines respectively."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06512","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:48:00Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:48:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:48:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06504","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06504","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06504","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingxuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Qingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyang","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Zhiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Chuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Qiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjie","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Text2Interact: High-Fidelity and Diverse Text-to-Two-Person Interaction Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T22:41:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:12:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modeling human-human interactions from text remains challenging because it requires not only realistic individual dynamics but also precise, text-consistent spatiotemporal coupling between agents. Currently, progress is hindered by 1) limited two-person training data, inadequate to capture the diverse intricacies of two-person interactions; and 2) insufficiently fine-grained text-to-interaction modeling, where language conditioning collapses rich, structured prompts into a single sentence embedding. To address these limitations, we propose our Text2Interact framework, designed to generate realistic, text-aligned human-human interactions through a scalable high-fidelity interaction data synthesizer and an effective spatiotemporal coordination pipeline. First, we present InterCompose, a scalable synthesis-by-composition pipeline that aligns LLM-generated interaction descriptions with strong single-person motion priors. Given a prompt and a motion for an agent, InterCompose retrieves candidate single-person motions, trains a conditional reaction generator for another agent, and uses a neural motion evaluator to filter weak or misaligned samples-expanding interaction coverage without extra capture. Second, we propose InterActor, a text-to-interaction model with word-level conditioning that preserves token-level cues (initiation, response, contact ordering) and an adaptive interaction loss that emphasizes contextually relevant inter-person joint pairs, improving coupling and physical plausibility for fine-grained interaction modeling. Extensive experiments show consistent gains in motion diversity, fidelity, and generalization, including out-of-distribution scenarios and user studies. We will release code and models to facilitate reproducibility."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06504","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:47:49Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:47:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:47:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06475","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06475","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06475","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yitao","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Yitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuru","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yuru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongjun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingchen","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqiu","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yiqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arman","familyName":"Cohan","name":"Cohan, Arman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dennis","familyName":"Shasha","name":"Shasha, Dennis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PuzzlePlex: Benchmarking Foundation Models on Reasoning and Planning with Puzzles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T21:24:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:10:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work investigates the reasoning and planning capabilities of foundation models and their scalability in complex, dynamic environments. We introduce PuzzlePlex, a benchmark designed to assess these capabilities through a diverse set of puzzles. PuzzlePlex consists of 15 types of puzzles, including deterministic and stochastic games of varying difficulty, as well as single-player and two-player scenarios. The PuzzlePlex framework provides a comprehensive environment for each game, and supports extensibility to generate more challenging instances as foundation models evolve. Additionally, we implement customized game-playing strategies for comparison. Building on this benchmark, we develop fine-grained metrics to measure performance and conduct an in-depth analysis of frontier foundation models across two settings: instruction-based and code-based. Furthermore, we systematically investigate their scaling limits. Our findings show that reasoning models outperform others in instruction-based settings, while code-based execution presents greater challenges but offers a scalable and efficient alternative. PuzzlePlex enables targeted evaluation and guides future improvements in reasoning, planning, and generalization for foundation models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06475","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:47:09Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:47:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:47:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06430","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06430","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06430","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neeraja","familyName":"Kirtane","name":"Kirtane, Neeraja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuvraj","familyName":"Khanna","name":"Khanna, Yuvraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Relan","name":"Relan, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MathRobust-LV: Evaluation of Large Language Models' Robustness to Linguistic Variations in Mathematical Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T20:09:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:07:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models excel on math benchmarks, but their math reasoning robustness to linguistic variation is underexplored. While recent work increasingly treats high-difficulty competitions like the IMO as the gold standard for evaluating reasoning, we believe in comprehensive benchmarking of high school-level math problems in real educational settings. We introduce MathRobust-LV, a test set and evaluation methodology that mirrors how instructors rephrase problems across assessments while keeping difficulty constant: we change surface details (names, contexts, variables) while preserving numerical structure and answers. In contrast to prior efforts that alter problem content or emphasize IMO-level tasks, we focus on high-school-level dataset problems at the difficulty level where models are currently deployed in educational settings: tutoring and assessment systems. In these applications, instructors rephrase identical concepts in varied ways, making linguistic robustness essential for reliable deployment. Although MATH data benchmarking is often regarded as saturated, our experiment on 34 models reveals that accuracy declines when moving from the baseline to the variants. These drops are severe for smaller models (9-11%) while stronger models also show measurable degradation. Frontier models like GPT-5, Gemini-2.5pro remain comparatively stable. Our results highlight that robustness to linguistic variation is a fundamental challenge, exposing reasoning vulnerabilities in models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06430","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:46:06Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:46:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:46:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06426","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06426","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06426","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yitao","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Yitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiansheng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Tiansheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arman","familyName":"Cohan","name":"Cohan, Arman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FinLFQA: Evaluating Attributed Text Generation of LLMs in Financial Long-Form Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T20:06:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:07:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) frequently hallucinate to long-form questions, producing plausible yet factually incorrect answers. A common mitigation strategy is to provide attribution to LLM outputs. However, existing benchmarks primarily focus on simple attribution that retrieves supporting textual evidence as references. We argue that in real-world scenarios such as financial applications, attribution goes beyond reference retrieval. We introduce FinLFQA, a benchmark designed to evaluate the ability of LLMs to generate long-form answers to complex financial questions with reliable and nuanced attributions. FinLFQA evaluates three critical aspects of attribution through human annotations: (1) supporting evidence extracted from financial reports, (2) intermediate numerical reasoning steps, and (3) domain-specific financial knowledge that informs the reasoning process. We further provide an automatic evaluation framework covering both answer quality and attribution quality. Through extensive experiments on eight LLMs across multiple attribution-generation paradigms, we find that fine-grained metrics are important to distinguish model capabilities, that end-to-end generation achieves comparable performance to post-hoc approaches, and that iterative refinement only helps when guided by external feedback."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06426","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:46:00Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:46:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:46:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06357","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06357","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06357","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grayson","familyName":"Byrd","name":"Byrd, Grayson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Corban","familyName":"Rivera","name":"Rivera, Corban","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bethany","familyName":"Kemp","name":"Kemp, Bethany","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meghan","familyName":"Booker","name":"Booker, Meghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aurora","familyName":"Schmidt","name":"Schmidt, Aurora","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Celso M","familyName":"de Melo","name":"de Melo, Celso M","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lalithkumar","familyName":"Seenivasan","name":"Seenivasan, Lalithkumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathias","familyName":"Unberath","name":"Unberath, Mathias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constrained Natural Language Action Planning for Resilient Embodied Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:23:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:03:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Replicating human-level intelligence in the execution of embodied tasks remains challenging due to the unconstrained nature of real-world environments. Novel use of large language models (LLMs) for task planning seeks to address the previously intractable state/action space of complex planning tasks, but hallucinations limit their reliability, and thus, viability beyond a research context. Additionally, the prompt engineering required to achieve adequate system performance lacks transparency, and thus, repeatability. In contrast to LLM planning, symbolic planning methods offer strong reliability and repeatability guarantees, but struggle to scale to the complexity and ambiguity of real-world tasks. We introduce a new robotic planning method that augments LLM planners with symbolic planning oversight to improve reliability and repeatability, and provide a transparent approach to defining hard constraints with considerably stronger clarity than traditional prompt engineering. Importantly, these augmentations preserve the reasoning capabilities of LLMs and retain impressive generalization in open-world environments. We demonstrate our approach in simulated and real-world environments. On the ALFWorld planning benchmark, our approach outperforms current state-of-the-art methods, achieving a near-perfect 99% success rate. Deployment of our method to a real-world quadruped robot resulted in 100% task success compared to 50% and 30% for pure LLM and symbolic planners across embodied pick and place tasks. Our approach presents an effective strategy to enhance the reliability, repeatability and transparency of LLM-based robot planners while retaining their key strengths: flexibility and generalizability to complex real-world environments. We hope that this work will contribute to the broad goal of building resilient embodied intelligent systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06357","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:25Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:44:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06274","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06274","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06274","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Mahdi Samiei","familyName":"Paqaleh","name":"Paqaleh, Mohammad Mahdi Samiei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arash","familyName":"Marioriyad","name":"Marioriyad, Arash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arman","familyName":"Tahmasebi-Zadeh","name":"Tahmasebi-Zadeh, Arman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamadreza","familyName":"Fereydooni","name":"Fereydooni, Mohamadreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahdi","familyName":"Ghaznavai","name":"Ghaznavai, Mahdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahdieh Soleymani","familyName":"Baghshah","name":"Baghshah, Mahdieh Soleymani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging Reasoning to Learning: Unmasking Illusions using Complexity Out of Distribution Generalization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T13:08:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent progress has pushed AI frontiers from pattern recognition tasks toward problems that require step by step, System2 style reasoning, especially with large language models. Yet, unlike learning, where generalization and out of distribution (OoD) evaluation concepts are well formalized, there is no clear, consistent definition or metric for reasoning ability. We propose Complexity Out of Distribution (Complexity OoD) generalization as a framework and problem setting to define and measure reasoning. A model exhibits Complexity OoD generalization when it maintains performance on test instances whose minimal required solution complexity, either representational (richer solution structure) or computational (more reasoning steps/program length), exceeds that of all training examples. We formalize complexity via solution description Kolmogorov complexity and operational proxies (e.g., object/relation counts; reasoning step counts), clarifying how Complexity OoD differs from length and compositional OoD. This lens unifies learning and reasoning: many cases solvable with System1 like processing at low complexity become System2 like under complexity pressure, while System2 can be viewed as generalization over solution structures. We translate this perspective into practice with recommendations for operationalizing Complexity OoD across the stack: incorporating complexity into benchmark and evaluation metric design, rethinking supervision to target solution traces, seeking and designing inductive biases for Complexity OoD generalization, addressing learning to reason spillovers such as spurious shortcuts, semantic robustness, catastrophic forgetting, and step wise calibration. Because Complexity OoD cannot be solved by scaling data alone, progress toward robust reasoning will require architectures and training regimes that explicitly model and allocate computation with respect to complexity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06274","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:28Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:42:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":106,"totalPages":2,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-08T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-09T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%28agent+OR+agents+OR+reasoning+OR+planning+OR+memory+OR+retrieval+OR+RAG+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+%22tool+calling%22%29+AND+%28LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-08T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-09T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%28agent+OR+agents+OR+reasoning+OR+planning+OR+memory+OR+retrieval+OR+RAG+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+%22tool+calling%22%29+AND+%28LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29%29"}}